{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R6bACoqcKWCwWHAscJBBJ6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — gated_mfrm_judge_bias\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: AAAI 2027 (full paper, deadline July 28, 2026)\n5\t- Backup: ACL 2027 via ARR (submission August 3, 2026)\n6\t- Timeline: ~68 days from today (2026-05-21)\n7\t\n8\t## Core Insight\n9\tSequential approaches to judge bias correction and contamination adjustment produce biased model ability estimates due to omitted variable confounding. Joint estimation within a single psychometric calibration framework is methodologically necessary, not merely convenient.\n10\t\n11\t## Core Claims (3 条，均可证伪)\n12\t\n13\t1. **Confounding Claim**: When judge severity (d_j) and contamination susceptibility (c_n·e_i) are both present but only one is modeled, the unmodeled facet's effect is absorbed into other parameter estimates, producing systematic bias in model ability rankings. Sequential pipelines (bias-first or contamination-first) do not resolve this confounding. (Note: OVB formalization supports this claim as mechanism explanation — \"identify and quantify confounding in LLM evaluation\" — not as independent theoretical contribution. Cite Angrist & Pischke 2009 as source.)\n14\t\n15\t2. **Framework Claim**: The Gated Many-Facet Rasch Model (G-MFRM) — combining MFRM's multi-facet structure with DG-IRT's gating mechanism and externally-anchored exposure indicators — provides a staged joint calibration that estimates model ability, item difficulty, judge severity, and contamination susceptibility, yielding bias-and-contamination-corrected \"fair scores.\" (Note: \"staged joint\" — e_i is pre-estimated in Stage 2 and fixed in Stage 3; the joint estimation is conditional on e_i. This is a deliberate identifiability choice, not fully simultaneous. Paper must use \"staged joint calibration\" or \"conditional joint estimation,\" never \"fully joint.\")\n16\t\n17\t3. **Empirical Claim**: G-MFRM fair scores achieve higher agreement with human rankings (Chatbot Arena votes, Kendall-τ) than all baselines: raw multi-judge averages, bias-only correction (CyclicJudge, UDA), contamination-only adjustment (DCR), and naive sequential pipelines.\n18\t\n19\t## Story Spine\n20\t\n21\t- **Problem**: LLM-as-a-judge evaluation has bifurcated into two correction silos — judge bias and benchmark contamination — that are addressed independently. But judge severity and contamination effects are confounded: bias-only methods absorb contamination inflation into ability estimates; contamination-only methods misattribute judge effects as contamination signals. Sequential correction cannot resolve this because the first stage's estimates are already biased by the unmodeled facet.\n22\t\n23\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**: CyclicJudge eliminates average judge bias via round-robin but ignores contamination. UDA uses neural K-factor adjustment (−63.4% inter-judge std) but is black-box and contamination-unaware. DCR/ConStat quantify contamination effects (r²=0.94) but ignore judge variation. In LLM evaluation literature, no existing work jointly models both within a single calibration framework. (Note: educational measurement has MFRM+DIF joint modeling — Eckes 2011, Patz et al. 2002, Fox & Glas 2001, Wind & Peterson 2018 — but these address test fairness, not LLM contamination. G-MFRM is the first to apply continuous-exposure gating within MFRM for LLM evaluation. \"First\" claim must be precisely scoped to LLM evaluation with continuous exposure.)\n24\t\n25\t- **Our framing**: Joint estimation is methodologically necessary. We formalize this as an omitted variable bias problem: under non-orthogonality of d_j and c_n·e_i, sequential estimators are asymptotically biased (direction determined by covariance sign). G-MFRM resolves this by estimating all facets simultaneously, with a three-stage procedure ensuring identifiability: (1) standard MFRM for initial estimates, (2) external per-item exposure estimation via item-level performance gap (original vs reference variants) with difficulty correction, supplemented by temporal signals and LogProber where available, (3) joint gated model with e_i fixed and c_n as latent parameters. ConStat per-model delta serves as external validation of fitted c_n.\n26\t\n27\t- **Headline result template**: \"On MT-Bench/Arena-Hard with 8 LLM judges, G-MFRM fair scores achieve X% higher Kendall-τ with Chatbot Arena human votes than the best sequential baseline, while simultaneously producing interpretable per-model contamination estimates and per-judge severity profiles.\"\n28\t\n29\t## Narrative Strategy (from distilled writing)\n30\t\n31\t- **Type**: \"突破局限\" (sequential has structural flaw) + \"统一框架\" (G-MFRM unifies both corrections)\n32\t- **Opening hook**: Quantitative shock — lead with confounding's θ-RMSE degradation (43.8% in synthetic, to be validated on real data)\n33\t- **Section 2**: Toy example / formal **observation** (NOT \"proposition\" — this is standard OVB from Angrist & Pischke 2009 applied to LLM evaluation; cite originals, present as domain-specific instantiation) demonstrating confounding before introducing G-MFRM method\n34\t- **Figure 1**: Left = sequential pipeline with confounding annotation, Right = joint G-MFRM with gating mechanism highlighted\n35\t- **Main result table**: rows = methods (7 baselines + G-MFRM), cols = benchmarks × metrics\n36\t- **Ablation**: Full numbers (not checkmark grid), rows = components on/off\n37\t\n38\t## Venue Fit Risks\n39\t\n40\t- **AAAI**: Good fit for psychometric methodology applied to AI evaluation. Risk: reviewers may lack MFRM/IRT background — need clear, self-contained exposition with toy examples. Strength: AAAI values formal modeling + practical impact.\n41\t- **ACL/ARR**: Good fit for LLM evaluation methodology. Risk: may demand stronger NLP-specific contributions beyond the measurement framework. Strength: directly relevant to LLM evaluation community.\n42\t\n43\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Responses\n44\t\n45\t1. **\"Why not CyclicJudge + discard contaminated items?\"** — Item discard wastes information; CyclicJudge doesn't capture item-specific judge effects; neither produces c_n for auditing; G-MFRM degrades gracefully to standard MFRM when contamination ≈ 0.\n46\t\n47\t2. **\"Is confounding large enough in practice?\"** — MVP synthetic: 43.8% RMSE degradation. Real-data calibration (P0 experiment) will establish realistic contamination magnitude. Bias scatter (r=0.65) shows the mechanism exists regardless of magnitude.\n48\t\n49\t3. **\"Rank-1 assumption (c_n·e_i) too strong\"** — Rank-1 is LLTM tradition, good approximation when contamination comes from dominant data source. Person-fit diagnostics (infit/outfit) detect violations. Rank-k extension is future work.\n50\t\n51\t4. **\"DG-IRT transfer is trivial\"** — Three structural differences require non-trivial adaptation: binary→continuous exposure, single-rater→multi-facet, known→unknown exposure status. Stage 2 external anchoring solves a fundamental obstacle (exposure unknown to administrator) absent in educational testing. Error propagation from Stage 2→3 must be discussed in Limitations with sensitivity analysis (vary e_i noise σ=0.1,0.2,0.5).\n52\t\n53\t4b. **\"Staged estimation ≠ joint estimation\"** — Correct: e_i is pre-fixed in Stage 2, so estimation is conditional-joint, not fully simultaneous. Frame as deliberate identifiability choice: true joint estimation of (θ,b,d,c,e) is under-identified without external anchoring. Staged estimation is the principled solution. Compare to two-step estimators in econometrics (Heckman 1979). Error propagation sensitivity analysis shows robustness.\n54\t\n55\t5. **\"Contamination signal realistic?\"** — ConStat validated r²=0.94 with true contamination effects. LogProber provides per-item memorization indicators. Multiple indicators combined via weighted composite. P0 experiment calibrates against real benchmarks.\n56\t\n57\t6. **\"Chatbot Arena Elo as ground truth — circular reasoning?\"** — Arena uses real-time human pairwise comparisons with user-submitted prompts (not fixed benchmark items). Contamination inflates benchmark scores but does not directly improve conversational ability. Arena Elo is the least-contaminated human preference signal available. Acknowledge limitation in paper but argue decoupling is strong.\n58\t\n59\t7. **\"c_n ≥ 0 assumption too restrictive?\"** — Half-Cauchy prior enforces non-negativity, appropriate for the \"contamination as unfair advantage\" framing. Adversarial contamination (intentional performance degradation) is outside scope. Discuss in Limitations.\n60\t\n61\t8. **\"≥4 judges needed — too expensive?\"** — exp-006 sensitivity analysis shows degradation curve. Discuss in Limitations: at 2 judges, G-MFRM degrades gracefully to MFRM-level (no contamination correction). Cost-benefit analysis for practitioners.\n62\t\n63\t9. **\"No judge-model interaction term?\"** — G-MFRM has d_j (judge-level) and c_n·e_i (model-item), but no f_{nj} (judge-model interaction). Preference Leakage shows same-model PLS≈23.6%, same-family≈8.9%. When GPT-5.5 judges GPT-5.5, this bias distributes uniformly across items and gets absorbed into θ̂_n. Mitigation: (a) exp-006 leave-one-judge-out residual diagnostic detects if specific judges inflate same-family models; (b) Limitations section acknowledges scope boundary; (c) rank-k extension with judge-model interaction is future work.\n64\t\n65\t## Two-Tier Narrative (contingent on exp-005 contamination magnitude)\n66\t\n67\t**Tier A — Strong contamination (c ≈ 1.0-1.5, matching mvp_002/003)**:\n68\t- Lead with ranking correction as primary contribution\n69\t- Claim 3 at full strength: \"G-MFRM achieves X% higher Kendall-τ\"\n70\t- Headline: joint estimation dramatically outperforms sequential\n71\t\n72\t**Tier B — Weak contamination (c ≈ 0.3-0.5, matching mvp_001)**:\n73\t- Elevate diagnostic interpretability to co-primary contribution (per IDEA_BRIEF optimization A4)\n74\t- Reframe: \"even when ranking improvement is modest, G-MFRM uniquely provides per-model c_n (training audit), per-item e_i (item retirement), per-judge d_j (judge calibration)\"\n75\t- Bias scatter r=0.65 proves the mechanism exists; practical impact scales with contamination magnitude\n76\t- Claim 3 softened to \"comparable or better rankings PLUS interpretable diagnostic outputs\"\n77\t\n78\t**Tier B+ — Diagnostics as sole primary (if Claim 1 ρ≈0 AND Claim 3 Δτ non-significant)**:\n79\t- Diagnostics become the **sole** primary contribution (not co-primary)\n80\t- Paper becomes \"tool paper\": G-MFRM as diagnostic framework for LLM evaluation auditing\n81\t- Contribution: per-model c_n (training data audit), per-item e_i (item retirement), per-judge d_j (judge calibration) — unified diagnostic outputs no existing tool provides\n82\t- Risk: \"tool paper\" positioning may be weaker for Main Conference; strong for Findings\n83\t\n84\texp-005 determines which tier. Decision point: after exp-005 results, before paper writing.\n85\t\n86\t## Novelty Supplementation Strategy\n87\t\n88\tDream R2 exposed a novelty ceiling: \"MFRM + 1 term\" + \"textbook OVB\" = combinatorial novelty. Even with best-case data, Main Conference bar may not be met on novelty alone. Supplement with:\n89\t\n90\t1. **Identifiability analysis** (Method section): Prove necessary and sufficient conditions for (θ, b, d, c) identifiability when e_i is fixed. This is a non-trivial technical contribution beyond \"adding a term.\"\n91\t2. **Formal e_i uncertainty propagation**: Beyond sensitivity analysis (exp-012), provide theoretical treatment of how e_i estimation error propagates to c_n credible intervals. Compare to two-step estimator literature (Heckman 1979, Murphy & Topel 1985).\n92\t3. **Diagnostic framework formalization**: Frame the four-dimensional output (θ, d_j, c_n, e_i) as a principled measurement framework, not just parameter estimates. Connect to DIF (Differential Item Functioning) tradition.\n93\t\n94\t## Bibliography Strategy (Paper Agent input)\n95\t\n96\tRequired references by positioning role:\n97\t- **Foundational IRT**: Rasch 1960, Lord & Novick 1968, Fox 2010 (Bayesian IRT)\n98\t- **MFRM ancestor**: Linacre 1989, Engelhard 2002/2013, Eckes 2011/2015\n99\t- **DG-IRT ancestor**: Shu et al. 2013 (Psychometrika), Robitzsch 2020\n100\t- **MFRM+DIF (educational)**: Wind & Peterson 2018, Patz et al. 2002, Fox & Glas 2001\n101\t- **LLM contamination**: Golchin & Surdeanu 2023, Shi et al. 2023 (Min-K%), ConStat, DCR\n102\t- **LLM judge bias**: Zheng et al. 2023 (MT-Bench), CyclicJudge, UDA, Preference Leakage\n103\t- **Benchmarks**: Arena-Hard original, WildBench original, LiveBench\n104\t- **Econometrics**: Angrist & Pischke 2009 (OVB), Heckman 1979 (two-step)\n105\tTarget: 30-40 references total.\n106\t\n107\t## MVP Evidence Summary\n108\t\n109\t- mvp_003: Joint RMSE −43.8% vs Sequential (p=1e-6), Kendall-τ +6.3pp, R̂=1.03, ESS=224\n110\t- Bias scatter: Pearson r=0.65 (sequential systematically overestimates high-c_n models)\n111\t- Robustness: method effective even under model misspecification (DGP with s_j, model without)\n112\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0ac9dd48-cd85-42c4-ac70-70acb66bc713","uuid":"8dd012d0-1e84-48a6-8f8d-52063b474079","timestamp":"2026-05-22T04:47:21.974Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-prod43/projects/gated_mfrm_judge_bias/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T041217/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — gated_mfrm_judge_bias\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: AAAI 2027 (full paper, deadline July 28, 2026)\n- Backup: ACL 2027 via ARR (submission August 3, 2026)\n- Timeline: ~68 days from today (2026-05-21)\n\n## Core Insight\nSequential approaches to judge bias correction and contamination adjustment produce biased model ability estimates due to omitted variable confounding. Joint estimation within a single psychometric calibration framework is methodologically necessary, not merely convenient.\n\n## Core Claims (3 条，均可证伪)\n\n1. **Confounding Claim**: When judge severity (d_j) and contamination susceptibility (c_n·e_i) are both present but only one is modeled, the unmodeled facet's effect is absorbed into other parameter estimates, producing systematic bias in model ability rankings. Sequential pipelines (bias-first or contamination-first) do not resolve this confounding. (Note: OVB formalization supports this claim as mechanism explanation — \"identify and quantify confounding in LLM evaluation\" — not as independent theoretical contribution. Cite Angrist & Pischke 2009 as source.)\n\n2. **Framework Claim**: The Gated Many-Facet Rasch Model (G-MFRM) — combining MFRM's multi-facet structure with DG-IRT's gating mechanism and externally-anchored exposure indicators — provides a staged joint calibration that estimates model ability, item difficulty, judge severity, and contamination susceptibility, yielding bias-and-contamination-corrected \"fair scores.\" (Note: \"staged joint\" — e_i is pre-estimated in Stage 2 and fixed in Stage 3; the joint estimation is conditional on e_i. This is a deliberate identifiability choice, not fully simultaneous. Paper must use \"staged joint calibration\" or \"conditional joint estimation,\" never \"fully joint.\")\n\n3. **Empirical Claim**: G-MFRM fair scores achieve higher agreement with human rankings (Chatbot Arena votes, Kendall-τ) than all baselines: raw multi-judge averages, bias-only correction (CyclicJudge, UDA), contamination-only adjustment (DCR), and naive sequential pipelines.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n- **Problem**: LLM-as-a-judge evaluation has bifurcated into two correction silos — judge bias and benchmark contamination — that are addressed independently. But judge severity and contamination effects are confounded: bias-only methods absorb contamination inflation into ability estimates; contamination-only methods misattribute judge effects as contamination signals. Sequential correction cannot resolve this because the first stage's estimates are already biased by the unmodeled facet.\n\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: CyclicJudge eliminates average judge bias via round-robin but ignores contamination. UDA uses neural K-factor adjustment (−63.4% inter-judge std) but is black-box and contamination-unaware. DCR/ConStat quantify contamination effects (r²=0.94) but ignore judge variation. In LLM evaluation literature, no existing work jointly models both within a single calibration framework. (Note: educational measurement has MFRM+DIF joint modeling — Eckes 2011, Patz et al. 2002, Fox & Glas 2001, Wind & Peterson 2018 — but these address test fairness, not LLM contamination. G-MFRM is the first to apply continuous-exposure gating within MFRM for LLM evaluation. \"First\" claim must be precisely scoped to LLM evaluation with continuous exposure.)\n\n- **Our framing**: Joint estimation is methodologically necessary. We formalize this as an omitted variable bias problem: under non-orthogonality of d_j and c_n·e_i, sequential estimators are asymptotically biased (direction determined by covariance sign). G-MFRM resolves this by estimating all facets simultaneously, with a three-stage procedure ensuring identifiability: (1) standard MFRM for initial estimates, (2) external per-item exposure estimation via item-level performance gap (original vs reference variants) with difficulty correction, supplemented by temporal signals and LogProber where available, (3) joint gated model with e_i fixed and c_n as latent parameters. ConStat per-model delta serves as external validation of fitted c_n.\n\n- **Headline result template**: \"On MT-Bench/Arena-Hard with 8 LLM judges, G-MFRM fair scores achieve X% higher Kendall-τ with Chatbot Arena human votes than the best sequential baseline, while simultaneously producing interpretable per-model contamination estimates and per-judge severity profiles.\"\n\n## Narrative Strategy (from distilled writing)\n\n- **Type**: \"突破局限\" (sequential has structural flaw) + \"统一框架\" (G-MFRM unifies both corrections)\n- **Opening hook**: Quantitative shock — lead with confounding's θ-RMSE degradation (43.8% in synthetic, to be validated on real data)\n- **Section 2**: Toy example / formal **observation** (NOT \"proposition\" — this is standard OVB from Angrist & Pischke 2009 applied to LLM evaluation; cite originals, present as domain-specific instantiation) demonstrating confounding before introducing G-MFRM method\n- **Figure 1**: Left = sequential pipeline with confounding annotation, Right = joint G-MFRM with gating mechanism highlighted\n- **Main result table**: rows = methods (7 baselines + G-MFRM), cols = benchmarks × metrics\n- **Ablation**: Full numbers (not checkmark grid), rows = components on/off\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n\n- **AAAI**: Good fit for psychometric methodology applied to AI evaluation. Risk: reviewers may lack MFRM/IRT background — need clear, self-contained exposition with toy examples. Strength: AAAI values formal modeling + practical impact.\n- **ACL/ARR**: Good fit for LLM evaluation methodology. Risk: may demand stronger NLP-specific contributions beyond the measurement framework. Strength: directly relevant to LLM evaluation community.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Responses\n\n1. **\"Why not CyclicJudge + discard contaminated items?\"** — Item discard wastes information; CyclicJudge doesn't capture item-specific judge effects; neither produces c_n for auditing; G-MFRM degrades gracefully to standard MFRM when contamination ≈ 0.\n\n2. **\"Is confounding large enough in practice?\"** — MVP synthetic: 43.8% RMSE degradation. Real-data calibration (P0 experiment) will establish realistic contamination magnitude. Bias scatter (r=0.65) shows the mechanism exists regardless of magnitude.\n\n3. **\"Rank-1 assumption (c_n·e_i) too strong\"** — Rank-1 is LLTM tradition, good approximation when contamination comes from dominant data source. Person-fit diagnostics (infit/outfit) detect violations. Rank-k extension is future work.\n\n4. **\"DG-IRT transfer is trivial\"** — Three structural differences require non-trivial adaptation: binary→continuous exposure, single-rater→multi-facet, known→unknown exposure status. Stage 2 external anchoring solves a fundamental obstacle (exposure unknown to administrator) absent in educational testing. Error propagation from Stage 2→3 must be discussed in Limitations with sensitivity analysis (vary e_i noise σ=0.1,0.2,0.5).\n\n4b. **\"Staged estimation ≠ joint estimation\"** — Correct: e_i is pre-fixed in Stage 2, so estimation is conditional-joint, not fully simultaneous. Frame as deliberate identifiability choice: true joint estimation of (θ,b,d,c,e) is under-identified without external anchoring. Staged estimation is the principled solution. Compare to two-step estimators in econometrics (Heckman 1979). Error propagation sensitivity analysis shows robustness.\n\n5. **\"Contamination signal realistic?\"** — ConStat validated r²=0.94 with true contamination effects. LogProber provides per-item memorization indicators. Multiple indicators combined via weighted composite. P0 experiment calibrates against real benchmarks.\n\n6. **\"Chatbot Arena Elo as ground truth — circular reasoning?\"** — Arena uses real-time human pairwise comparisons with user-submitted prompts (not fixed benchmark items). Contamination inflates benchmark scores but does not directly improve conversational ability. Arena Elo is the least-contaminated human preference signal available. Acknowledge limitation in paper but argue decoupling is strong.\n\n7. **\"c_n ≥ 0 assumption too restrictive?\"** — Half-Cauchy prior enforces non-negativity, appropriate for the \"contamination as unfair advantage\" framing. Adversarial contamination (intentional performance degradation) is outside scope. Discuss in Limitations.\n\n8. **\"≥4 judges needed — too expensive?\"** — exp-006 sensitivity analysis shows degradation curve. Discuss in Limitations: at 2 judges, G-MFRM degrades gracefully to MFRM-level (no contamination correction). Cost-benefit analysis for practitioners.\n\n9. **\"No judge-model interaction term?\"** — G-MFRM has d_j (judge-level) and c_n·e_i (model-item), but no f_{nj} (judge-model interaction). Preference Leakage shows same-model PLS≈23.6%, same-family≈8.9%. When GPT-5.5 judges GPT-5.5, this bias distributes uniformly across items and gets absorbed into θ̂_n. Mitigation: (a) exp-006 leave-one-judge-out residual diagnostic detects if specific judges inflate same-family models; (b) Limitations section acknowledges scope boundary; (c) rank-k extension with judge-model interaction is future work.\n\n## Two-Tier Narrative (contingent on exp-005 contamination magnitude)\n\n**Tier A — Strong contamination (c ≈ 1.0-1.5, matching mvp_002/003)**:\n- Lead with ranking correction as primary contribution\n- Claim 3 at full strength: \"G-MFRM achieves X% higher Kendall-τ\"\n- Headline: joint estimation dramatically outperforms sequential\n\n**Tier B — Weak contamination (c ≈ 0.3-0.5, matching mvp_001)**:\n- Elevate diagnostic interpretability to co-primary contribution (per IDEA_BRIEF optimization A4)\n- Reframe: \"even when ranking improvement is modest, G-MFRM uniquely provides per-model c_n (training audit), per-item e_i (item retirement), per-judge d_j (judge calibration)\"\n- Bias scatter r=0.65 proves the mechanism exists; practical impact scales with contamination magnitude\n- Claim 3 softened to \"comparable or better rankings PLUS interpretable diagnostic outputs\"\n\n**Tier B+ — Diagnostics as sole primary (if Claim 1 ρ≈0 AND Claim 3 Δτ non-significant)**:\n- Diagnostics become the **sole** primary contribution (not co-primary)\n- Paper becomes \"tool paper\": G-MFRM as diagnostic framework for LLM evaluation auditing\n- Contribution: per-model c_n (training data audit), per-item e_i (item retirement), per-judge d_j (judge calibration) — unified diagnostic outputs no existing tool provides\n- Risk: \"tool paper\" positioning may be weaker for Main Conference; strong for Findings\n\nexp-005 determines which tier. Decision point: after exp-005 results, before paper writing.\n\n## Novelty Supplementation Strategy\n\nDream R2 exposed a novelty ceiling: \"MFRM + 1 term\" + \"textbook OVB\" = combinatorial novelty. Even with best-case data, Main Conference bar may not be met on novelty alone. Supplement with:\n\n1. **Identifiability analysis** (Method section): Prove necessary and sufficient conditions for (θ, b, d, c) identifiability when e_i is fixed. This is a non-trivial technical contribution beyond \"adding a term.\"\n2. **Formal e_i uncertainty propagation**: Beyond sensitivity analysis (exp-012), provide theoretical treatment of how e_i estimation error propagates to c_n credible intervals. Compare to two-step estimator literature (Heckman 1979, Murphy & Topel 1985).\n3. **Diagnostic framework formalization**: Frame the four-dimensional output (θ, d_j, c_n, e_i) as a principled measurement framework, not just parameter estimates. Connect to DIF (Differential Item Functioning) tradition.\n\n## Bibliography Strategy (Paper Agent input)\n\nRequired references by positioning role:\n- **Foundational IRT**: Rasch 1960, Lord & Novick 1968, Fox 2010 (Bayesian IRT)\n- **MFRM ancestor**: Linacre 1989, Engelhard 2002/2013, Eckes 2011/2015\n- **DG-IRT ancestor**: Shu et al. 2013 (Psychometrika), Robitzsch 2020\n- **MFRM+DIF (educational)**: Wind & Peterson 2018, Patz et al. 2002, Fox & Glas 2001\n- **LLM contamination**: Golchin & Surdeanu 2023, Shi et al. 2023 (Min-K%), ConStat, DCR\n- **LLM judge bias**: Zheng et al. 2023 (MT-Bench), CyclicJudge, UDA, Preference Leakage\n- **Benchmarks**: Arena-Hard original, WildBench original, LiveBench\n- **Econometrics**: Angrist & Pischke 2009 (OVB), Heckman 1979 (two-step)\nTarget: 30-40 references total.\n\n## MVP Evidence Summary\n\n- mvp_003: Joint RMSE −43.8% vs Sequential (p=1e-6), Kendall-τ +6.3pp, R̂=1.03, ESS=224\n- Bias scatter: Pearson r=0.65 (sequential systematically overestimates high-c_n models)\n- Robustness: method effective even under model misspecification (DGP with s_j, model without)\n","numLines":112,"startLine":1,"totalLines":112}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MzwQufqmCVeZMVKJyxxmzx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"multi_judge_data_collection\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Collect multi-judge evaluation data on MT-Bench and Arena-Hard with 8 judges × 20+ target models\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"total_judges_collected\": 8,\n7\t      \"total_target_models\": 22,\n8\t      \"total_judgments_mtbench\": 14080,\n9\t      \"total_judgments_arenahard\": 35200,\n10\t      \"position_swap_agreement\": 0.72,\n11\t      \"inter_judge_agreement_fleiss_kappa\": 0.38,\n12\t      \"judge_severity_range_raw\": 0.85,\n13\t      \"missing_data_pct\": 3.2,\n14\t      \"api_cost_usd\": 870,\n15\t      \"gpu_hours_used\": 118\n16\t    },\n17\t    \"tables\": [\n18\t      {\n19\t        \"name\": \"Judge Collection Summary\",\n20\t        \"columns\": [\n21\t          \"Judge\",\n22\t          \"N_judgments\",\n23\t          \"Mean_score\",\n24\t          \"SD\",\n25\t          \"Severity_range\",\n26\t          \"Position_bias\"\n27\t        ],\n28\t        \"rows\": [\n29\t          [\n30\t            \"GPT-5.5\",\n31\t            4400,\n32\t            7.82,\n33\t            1.21,\n34\t            \"ref\",\n35\t            0.08\n36\t          ],\n37\t          [\n38\t            \"Claude Opus 4.7\",\n39\t            4400,\n40\t            7.45,\n41\t            1.35,\n42\t            -0.37,\n43\t            0.12\n44\t          ],\n45\t          [\n46\t            \"Claude Sonnet 4.6\",\n47\t            4400,\n48\t            7.61,\n49\t            1.18,\n50\t            -0.21,\n51\t            0.09\n52\t          ],\n53\t          [\n54\t            \"DeepSeek-V4-Pro\",\n55\t            4400,\n56\t            7.28,\n57\t            1.42,\n58\t            -0.54,\n59\t            0.15\n60\t          ],\n61\t          [\n62\t            \"Qwen3-235B-A22B\",\n63\t            4400,\n64\t            7.53,\n65\t            1.08,\n66\t            -0.29,\n67\t            0.11\n68\t          ],\n69\t          [\n70\t            \"Qwen3.6-27B\",\n71\t            4400,\n72\t            7.91,\n73\t            0.82,\n74\t            0.09,\n75\t            0.06\n76\t          ],\n77\t          [\n78\t            \"Llama 4 Maverick\",\n79\t            4400,\n80\t            7.15,\n81\t            1.48,\n82\t            -0.67,\n83\t            0.14\n84\t          ],\n85\t          [\n86\t            \"Gemini 2.5 Pro\",\n87\t            4400,\n88\t            7.68,\n89\t            1.25,\n90\t            -0.14,\n91\t            0.1\n92\t          ]\n93\t        ]\n94\t      }\n95\t    ],\n96\t    \"figures\": [],\n97\t    \"analysis\": \"Data quality is adequate for MFRM fitting but the score distributions already hint at worst-tier outcome: no judge shows dramatically different severity for known-contaminated vs clean models. The compressed scoring range of Qwen3.6-27B (SD=0.82) will limit its contribution to facet separation.\\n\\nData collection itself is scenario-independent — the judges produce scores regardless of whether contamination exists. However, under worst tier the inter-judge agreement is moderate (κ≈0.38) because judges disagree primarily on difficulty/style rather than contamination-driven inflation. Position swap agreement is typical (0.72) indicating normal positional bias. The raw judge severity range (0.85 logits) is present but moderate — some judges (e.g., Qwen3.6-27B) show compressed scoring ranges (<0.3 logit range), limiting MFRM's value for those facets.\"\n98\t  },\n99\t  {\n100\t    \"name\": \"item_exposure_estimation\",\n101\t    \"description\": \"Estimate per-item exposure e_i via three-source pipeline (performance gap, temporal, LogProber)\",\n102\t    \"results\": {\n103\t      \"ei_mean\": 0.18,\n104\t      \"ei_median\": 0.12,\n105\t      \"ei_std\": 0.22,\n106\t      \"ei_max\": 0.71,\n107\t      \"ei_iqr\": [\n108\t        0.04,\n109\t        0.28\n110\t      ],\n111\t      \"pct_items_ei_above_0.5\": 8.5,\n112\t      \"performance_gap_signal_snr\": 1.2,\n113\t      \"temporal_signal_coverage_pct\": 35,\n114\t      \"logprober_coverage_pct\": 45,\n115\t      \"source_intercorrelation_gap_temporal\": 0.28,\n116\t      \"source_intercorrelation_gap_logprober\": 0.31,\n117\t      \"source_intercorrelation_temporal_logprober\": 0.19,\n118\t      \"weighted_composite_internal_consistency_alpha\": 0.24,\n119\t      \"spline_correction_r2\": 0.42,\n120\t      \"reference_variant_quality_check_pass_pct\": 78\n121\t    },\n122\t    \"tables\": [\n123\t      {\n124\t        \"name\": \"Exposure Source Contribution\",\n125\t        \"columns\": [\n126\t          \"Source\",\n127\t          \"Coverage_pct\",\n128\t          \"Mean_signal\",\n129\t          \"SD\",\n130\t          \"SNR\",\n131\t          \"Correlation_with_composite\"\n132\t        ],\n133\t        \"rows\": [\n134\t          [\n135\t            \"Performance gap (primary)\",\n136\t            100,\n137\t            0.15,\n138\t            0.19,\n139\t            1.2,\n140\t            0.78\n141\t          ],\n142\t          [\n143\t            \"Temporal signal\",\n144\t            35,\n145\t            0.21,\n146\t            0.31,\n147\t            0.8,\n148\t            0.52\n149\t          ],\n150\t          [\n151\t            \"LogProber\",\n152\t            45,\n153\t            0.14,\n154\t            0.24,\n155\t            0.9,\n156\t            0.61\n157\t          ]\n158\t        ]\n159\t      },\n160\t      {\n161\t        \"name\": \"e_i Distribution by Benchmark\",\n162\t        \"columns\": [\n163\t          \"Benchmark\",\n164\t          \"N_items\",\n165\t          \"Median_ei\",\n166\t          \"Mean_ei\",\n167\t          \"Pct_above_0.5\",\n168\t          \"Max_ei\"\n169\t        ],\n170\t        \"rows\": [\n171\t          [\n172\t            \"MT-Bench\",\n173\t            80,\n174\t            0.14,\n175\t            0.2,\n176\t            10.0,\n177\t            0.71\n178\t          ],\n179\t          [\n180\t            \"Arena-Hard\",\n181\t            500,\n182\t            0.11,\n183\t            0.17,\n184\t            7.8,\n185\t            0.63\n186\t          ]\n187\t        ]\n188\t      }\n189\t    ],\n190\t    \"figures\": [],\n191\t    \"analysis\": \"The e_i pipeline's internal consistency (α=0.24) is a critical weakness. The signal is real but noisy — identifiable in aggregate but unreliable at per-item level. This means Stage 3 fitting will have limited power to separate c_n from noise in e_i. The low median (0.12) confirms that most benchmark items are effectively clean, leaving little contamination signal for G-MFRM to exploit.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, e_i pipeline produces weak/noisy signal (effective σ_e ≈ 0.3–0.5 in the core assumption). Most items show near-zero exposure because current benchmarks (MT-Bench 2024 refresh, Arena-Hard v2) were designed to resist memorization. The three sources have low intercorrelation (0.19–0.31) indicating they capture different noise rather than converging on a true signal. Internal consistency α=0.24 is far below psychometric acceptability (0.70). The few items with e_i > 0.5 are likely legacy items from MT-Bench v1 that survived into the refresh. LogProber coverage is limited (45%) because many target models lack logprob APIs. The spline difficulty correction only explains 42% of variance in raw performance gaps — most of the gap is difficulty-driven, not contamination-driven.\"\n192\t  },\n193\t  {\n194\t    \"name\": \"gmfrm_real_data_fit\",\n195\t    \"description\": \"Fit G-MFRM three-stage pipeline on real data; compare fair scores with Chatbot Arena (Kendall-τ)\",\n196\t    \"results\": {\n197\t      \"stage1_mfrm_convergence_rhat_max\": 1.02,\n198\t      \"stage1_mfrm_ess_min\": 312,\n199\t      \"stage3_joint_convergence_rhat_max\": 1.12,\n200\t      \"stage3_joint_ess_min\": 58,\n201\t      \"stage3_cn_rhat_max\": 1.18,\n202\t      \"stage3_cn_ess_min\": 34,\n203\t      \"kendall_tau_arena_gmfrm_mtbench\": 0.76,\n204\t      \"kendall_tau_arena_gmfrm_arenahard\": 0.78,\n205\t      \"kendall_tau_arena_mfrm_only_mtbench\": 0.75,\n206\t      \"kendall_tau_arena_mfrm_only_arenahard\": 0.77,\n207\t      \"cn_median_fitted\": 0.15,\n208\t      \"cn_mean_fitted\": 0.22,\n209\t      \"cn_max_fitted\": 0.61,\n210\t      \"cn_95ci_width_median\": 0.48,\n211\t      \"theta_fair_vs_theta_mfrm_rank_correlation\": 0.97,\n212\t      \"pct_models_rank_change_geq_2\": 9.1,\n213\t      \"divergent_transitions_pct\": 2.8,\n214\t      \"runtime_hours\": 8.5\n215\t    },\n216\t    \"tables\": [\n217\t      {\n218\t        \"name\": \"G-MFRM Fit Summary by Benchmark\",\n219\t        \"columns\": [\n220\t          \"Benchmark\",\n221\t          \"R_hat_max\",\n222\t          \"ESS_min\",\n223\t          \"Divergent_pct\",\n224\t          \"cn_median\",\n225\t          \"cn_max\",\n226\t          \"tau_Arena\"\n227\t        ],\n228\t        \"rows\": [\n229\t          [\n230\t            \"MT-Bench\",\n231\t            1.12,\n232\t            58,\n233\t            2.8,\n234\t            0.17,\n235\t            0.61,\n236\t            0.76\n237\t          ],\n238\t          [\n239\t            \"Arena-Hard\",\n240\t            1.08,\n241\t            72,\n242\t            1.5,\n243\t            0.14,\n244\t            0.53,\n245\t            0.78\n246\t          ]\n247\t        ]\n248\t      },\n249\t      {\n250\t        \"name\": \"Fitted c_n Distribution (Top 5 Models)\",\n251\t        \"columns\": [\n252\t          \"Model\",\n253\t          \"c_n_mean\",\n254\t          \"c_n_95CI_low\",\n255\t          \"c_n_95CI_high\",\n256\t          \"CI_width\",\n257\t          \"R_hat\"\n258\t        ],\n259\t        \"rows\": [\n260\t          [\n261\t            \"Model_A (suspected legacy)\",\n262\t            0.61,\n263\t            0.18,\n264\t            1.12,\n265\t            0.94,\n266\t            1.15\n267\t          ],\n268\t          [\n269\t            \"Model_B\",\n270\t            0.43,\n271\t            0.08,\n272\t            0.89,\n273\t            0.81,\n274\t            1.18\n275\t          ],\n276\t          [\n277\t            \"Model_C\",\n278\t            0.35,\n279\t            0.05,\n280\t            0.74,\n281\t            0.69,\n282\t            1.11\n283\t          ],\n284\t          [\n285\t            \"Model_D\",\n286\t            0.22,\n287\t            0.01,\n288\t            0.58,\n289\t            0.57,\n290\t            1.09\n291\t          ],\n292\t          [\n293\t            \"Model_E\",\n294\t            0.19,\n295\t            0.01,\n296\t            0.52,\n297\t            0.51,\n298\t            1.07\n299\t          ]\n300\t        ]\n301\t      }\n302\t    ],\n303\t    \"figures\": [],\n304\t    \"analysis\": \"Stage 1 MFRM fits well (R̂=1.02) — judge severity and item difficulty are well-identified. But Stage 3 struggles with c_n: the combination of weak e_i signal and low true contamination means the data cannot distinguish c_n=0 from c_n=0.3 for most models. The 2.8% divergent transitions on MT-Bench indicate the posterior geometry is problematic near the c_n≈0 boundary. Practically, fair scores θ̂ are indistinguishable from MFRM-only estimates for 20/22 models. The G-MFRM 'correction' affects only 2-3 models with possible legacy contamination, and even those corrections are within posterior uncertainty.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, real c_n distribution has median < 0.3 (fitted median=0.15). Most models have near-zero contamination susceptibility — the c_n posterior piles up near the half-Cauchy prior's mode. The few models with c_n > 0.4 have wide credible intervals (median CI width=0.48) because e_i signal is weak. Stage 3 convergence is problematic: R̂=1.12 overall and up to 1.18 for c_n parameters specifically, with ESS as low as 34 for some c_n. This is because the data provides minimal information about c_n when e_i is near-zero for most items — the likelihood surface is nearly flat in the c_n direction. The resulting fair scores θ̂ barely differ from MFRM-only θ̂ (rank correlation 0.97), meaning the contamination correction has negligible practical effect. Kendall-τ with Arena is 0.76–0.78, within the expected range for this tier but not meaningfully better than MFRM-only.\"\n305\t  },\n306\t  {\n307\t    \"name\": \"baseline_comparison\",\n308\t    \"description\": \"Compare G-MFRM against 8 baselines; Kendall-τ and Spearman-ρ with Chatbot Arena; rank change analysis\",\n309\t    \"results\": {\n310\t      \"kendall_tau_raw_average\": 0.74,\n311\t      \"kendall_tau_mfrm_only\": 0.77,\n312\t      \"kendall_tau_sequential\": 0.76,\n313\t      \"kendall_tau_cyclicjudge\": 0.78,\n314\t      \"kendall_tau_cyclicjudge_discard\": 0.77,\n315\t      \"kendall_tau_dcr\": 0.75,\n316\t      \"kendall_tau_uda\": 0.79,\n317\t      \"kendall_tau_prefleak_calib\": 0.76,\n318\t      \"kendall_tau_gmfrm\": 0.77,\n319\t      \"best_baseline\": \"UDA\",\n320\t      \"delta_tau_vs_best_baseline\": -0.02,\n321\t      \"delta_tau_vs_mfrm_only\": 0.0,\n322\t      \"wilcoxon_p_gmfrm_vs_uda\": 0.34,\n323\t      \"wilcoxon_p_gmfrm_vs_cyclicjudge\": 0.62,\n324\t      \"spearman_rho_gmfrm\": 0.89,\n325\t      \"spearman_rho_best_baseline\": 0.91,\n326\t      \"rank_change_models_geq2_positions\": 2,\n327\t      \"top5_stability_gmfrm\": 0.8,\n328\t      \"top5_stability_best_baseline\": 0.8,\n329\t      \"post_hoc_power_for_observed_delta\": 0.12,\n330\t      \"n_models\": 22\n331\t    },\n332\t    \"tables\": [\n333\t      {\n334\t        \"name\": \"Main Comparison Table\",\n335\t        \"columns\": [\n336\t          \"Method\",\n337\t          \"Kendall_tau\",\n338\t          \"Spearman_rho\",\n339\t          \"Rank_changes_geq2\",\n340\t          \"Top5_stable\"\n341\t        ],\n342\t        \"rows\": [\n343\t          [\n344\t            \"Raw multi-judge average\",\n345\t            0.74,\n346\t            0.86,\n347\t            \"ref\",\n348\t            \"ref\"\n349\t          ],\n350\t          [\n351\t            \"MFRM-only (Stage 1)\",\n352\t            0.77,\n353\t            0.89,\n354\t            3,\n355\t            \"4/5\"\n356\t          ],\n357\t          [\n358\t            \"Sequential (MFRM→contamination)\",\n359\t            0.76,\n360\t            0.88,\n361\t            2,\n362\t            \"4/5\"\n363\t          ],\n364\t          [\n365\t            \"CyclicJudge\",\n366\t            0.78,\n367\t            0.9,\n368\t            3,\n369\t            \"4/5\"\n370\t          ],\n371\t          [\n372\t            \"CyclicJudge + item discard\",\n373\t            0.77,\n374\t            0.89,\n375\t            2,\n376\t            \"4/5\"\n377\t          ],\n378\t          [\n379\t            \"DCR Factor\",\n380\t            0.75,\n381\t            0.87,\n382\t            2,\n383\t            \"4/5\"\n384\t          ],\n385\t          [\n386\t            \"UDA\",\n387\t            0.79,\n388\t            0.91,\n389\t            4,\n390\t            \"4/5\"\n391\t          ],\n392\t          [\n393\t            \"Preference Leakage calibration\",\n394\t            0.76,\n395\t            0.88,\n396\t            2,\n397\t            \"4/5\"\n398\t          ],\n399\t          [\n400\t            \"G-MFRM (ours)\",\n401\t            0.77,\n402\t            0.89,\n403\t            2,\n404\t            \"4/5\"\n405\t          ]\n406\t        ]\n407\t      },\n408\t      {\n409\t        \"name\": \"Statistical Tests\",\n410\t        \"columns\": [\n411\t          \"Comparison\",\n412\t          \"Delta_tau\",\n413\t          \"Wilcoxon_p\",\n414\t          \"Power_at_alpha_0.05\",\n415\t          \"Significant?\"\n416\t        ],\n417\t        \"rows\": [\n418\t          [\n419\t            \"G-MFRM vs UDA\",\n420\t            -0.02,\n421\t            0.34,\n422\t            0.12,\n423\t            \"No\"\n424\t          ],\n425\t          [\n426\t            \"G-MFRM vs CyclicJudge\",\n427\t            -0.01,\n428\t            0.62,\n429\t            0.08,\n430\t            \"No\"\n431\t          ],\n432\t          [\n433\t            \"G-MFRM vs MFRM-only\",\n434\t            0.0,\n435\t            0.91,\n436\t            0.05,\n437\t            \"No\"\n438\t          ],\n439\t          [\n440\t            \"G-MFRM vs Sequential\",\n441\t            0.01,\n442\t            0.55,\n443\t            0.09,\n444\t            \"No\"\n445\t          ],\n446\t          [\n447\t            \"G-MFRM vs Raw average\",\n448\t            0.03,\n449\t            0.18,\n450\t            0.22,\n451\t            \"No\"\n452\t          ]\n453\t        ]\n454\t      }\n455\t    ],\n456\t    \"figures\": [],\n457\t    \"analysis\": \"Claim 3 is dead. G-MFRM does not improve over best baselines — it slightly underperforms UDA. Post-hoc power analysis (power=0.12) confirms the study is fundamentally underpowered to detect the observed effect size with N=22 models. Even with 50 models, power would be ~0.25 for Δτ=0.02. The ranking improvement is non-significant by any standard. MFRM-only and G-MFRM produce identical τ, confirming the contamination gating mechanism adds no signal. Pivot narrative: G-MFRM 'degrades gracefully' to MFRM level when contamination is absent — this is a feature, not a failure.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, Δτ is in [-0.02, 0.01] range. G-MFRM (τ=0.77) is actually slightly BELOW UDA (τ=0.79) — UDA's neural K-factor adjustment captures judge-specific biases that are the dominant source of ranking noise when contamination is negligible. CyclicJudge (τ=0.78) also edges out G-MFRM because round-robin bias elimination is effective when contamination is near-zero. The Wilcoxon test is massively underpowered (power=0.12 at N=22 models for detecting Δτ=0.02) — we cannot distinguish methods statistically. G-MFRM ties MFRM-only (both τ=0.77), confirming the contamination correction adds nothing when there's nothing to correct. Rank changes are minimal: only 2 models shift ≥2 positions between G-MFRM and best baseline, and these are within posterior uncertainty. This is the fatal result for Claim 3.\"\n458\t  },\n459\t  {\n460\t    \"name\": \"contamination_magnitude_calibration\",\n461\t    \"description\": \"Estimate real contamination magnitude; determine if c_n matches mvp_001 (weak) or mvp_002 (strong)\",\n462\t    \"results\": {\n463\t      \"real_cn_median\": 0.15,\n464\t      \"real_cn_mean\": 0.22,\n465\t      \"real_cn_max\": 0.61,\n466\t      \"real_cn_iqr\": [\n467\t        0.04,\n468\t        0.32\n469\t      ],\n470\t      \"pct_models_cn_above_0.5\": 9.1,\n471\t      \"pct_models_cn_above_1.0\": 0.0,\n472\t      \"closest_mvp_match\": \"below mvp_001\",\n473\t      \"mvp_001_cn_mean_was\": 0.5,\n474\t      \"ratio_real_to_mvp001\": 0.44,\n475\t      \"constat_delta_correlation_with_cn\": 0.32,\n476\t      \"constat_delta_median\": 0.08,\n477\t      \"cn_distribution_shape\": \"heavily right-skewed, mode near 0\",\n478\t      \"bimodality_coefficient\": 0.31\n479\t    },\n480\t    \"tables\": [\n481\t      {\n482\t        \"name\": \"Contamination Magnitude Comparison\",\n483\t        \"columns\": [\n484\t          \"Source\",\n485\t          \"cn_median\",\n486\t          \"cn_mean\",\n487\t          \"cn_max\",\n488\t          \"Interpretation\"\n489\t        ],\n490\t        \"rows\": [\n491\t          [\n492\t            \"MVP synthetic (mvp_001)\",\n493\t            0.5,\n494\t            0.5,\n495\t            1.0,\n496\t            \"Assumed moderate\"\n497\t          ],\n498\t          [\n499\t            \"MVP synthetic (mvp_002/003)\",\n500\t            1.5,\n501\t            1.5,\n502\t            2.5,\n503\t            \"Assumed strong\"\n504\t          ],\n505\t          [\n506\t            \"Real data (this experiment)\",\n507\t            0.15,\n508\t            0.22,\n509\t            0.61,\n510\t            \"Negligible to weak\"\n511\t          ]\n512\t        ]\n513\t      },\n514\t      {\n515\t        \"name\": \"Per-Model c_n Estimates\",\n516\t        \"columns\": [\n517\t          \"Model\",\n518\t          \"c_n_posterior_mean\",\n519\t          \"95CI\",\n520\t          \"ConStat_delta\",\n521\t          \"Category\"\n522\t        ],\n523\t        \"rows\": [\n524\t          [\n525\t            \"GPT-5.5\",\n526\t            0.08,\n527\t            \"[0.01, 0.29]\",\n528\t            0.05,\n529\t            \"negligible\"\n530\t          ],\n531\t          [\n532\t            \"Claude Opus 4.7\",\n533\t            0.05,\n534\t            \"[0.00, 0.21]\",\n535\t            0.03,\n536\t            \"negligible\"\n537\t          ],\n538\t          [\n539\t            \"Llama 4 405B\",\n540\t            0.35,\n541\t            \"[0.06, 0.72]\",\n542\t            0.15,\n543\t            \"weak\"\n544\t          ],\n545\t          [\n546\t            \"Qwen3-235B\",\n547\t            0.43,\n548\t            \"[0.08, 0.89]\",\n549\t            0.18,\n550\t            \"weak-moderate\"\n551\t          ],\n552\t          [\n553\t            \"DeepSeek-V4\",\n554\t            0.61,\n555\t            \"[0.18, 1.12]\",\n556\t            0.22,\n557\t            \"moderate (max)\"\n558\t          ]\n559\t        ]\n560\t      }\n561\t    ],\n562\t    \"figures\": [],\n563\t    \"analysis\": \"This is the definitive worst-tier signal. Real contamination is far below synthetic assumptions. The mvp_002/003 success (43.8% RMSE reduction) required c_n~1.5 — real data has c_n~0.15 (10× weaker). Even mvp_001 assumed c~0.5. The G-MFRM's gating mechanism is solving a problem that barely exists on these benchmarks. Pivot implications: (1) the null finding about contamination magnitude is itself publishable — benchmarks may be cleaner than assumed; (2) G-MFRM's value is as a diagnostic tool that CONFIRMS low contamination rather than correcting for high contamination; (3) need to find or construct a benchmark with known high contamination for validation case.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, real c_n median is in [0.05, 0.3] — we get 0.15, which is BELOW even mvp_001's assumption of c~0.5. The distribution is not bimodal but heavily right-skewed with mode near zero: most models have negligible contamination, 2-3 have moderate (0.4-0.6), none reach 1.0. This is well below the regime where G-MFRM's gating mechanism has meaningful leverage. The ConStat per-model delta validates only weakly (r=0.32) because both signals are near-floor — correlation is attenuated by floor effects and measurement noise. The finding itself IS informative: current benchmarks (MT-Bench 2024 refresh, Arena-Hard v2) appear to have effectively low contamination. This challenges assumptions in contamination literature that training data overlap produces large performance inflation on these specific benchmarks.\"\n564\t  },\n565\t  {\n566\t    \"name\": \"judge_count_sensitivity\",\n567\t    \"description\": \"Vary judges (2,4,6,8); measure G-MFRM degradation; leave-one-judge-out residual diagnostic\",\n568\t    \"results\": {\n569\t      \"tau_8_judges\": 0.77,\n570\t      \"tau_6_judges\": 0.76,\n571\t      \"tau_4_judges\": 0.75,\n572\t      \"tau_2_judges\": 0.73,\n573\t      \"tau_mfrm_only_8_judges\": 0.77,\n574\t      \"tau_mfrm_only_4_judges\": 0.75,\n575\t      \"delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_8j\": 0.0,\n576\t      \"delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_6j\": 0.0,\n577\t      \"delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_4j\": 0.0,\n578\t      \"delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_2j\": -0.01,\n579\t      \"cn_identifiability_threshold_judges\": \"not reached\",\n580\t      \"leave_one_out_max_residual_change\": 0.04,\n581\t      \"leave_one_out_same_family_bias_detected\": false,\n582\t      \"convergence_2j_rhat_max\": 1.25,\n583\t      \"convergence_4j_rhat_max\": 1.15,\n584\t      \"convergence_6j_rhat_max\": 1.1\n585\t    },\n586\t    \"tables\": [\n587\t      {\n588\t        \"name\": \"Judge Count Sensitivity\",\n589\t        \"columns\": [\n590\t          \"N_judges\",\n591\t          \"tau_GMFRM\",\n592\t          \"tau_MFRM\",\n593\t          \"Delta\",\n594\t          \"R_hat_max\",\n595\t          \"ESS_min_cn\"\n596\t        ],\n597\t        \"rows\": [\n598\t          [\n599\t            8,\n600\t            0.77,\n601\t            0.77,\n602\t            0.0,\n603\t            1.12,\n604\t            58\n605\t          ],\n606\t          [\n607\t            6,\n608\t            0.76,\n609\t            0.76,\n610\t            0.0,\n611\t            1.1,\n612\t            65\n613\t          ],\n614\t          [\n615\t            4,\n616\t            0.75,\n617\t            0.75,\n618\t            0.0,\n619\t            1.15,\n620\t            42\n621\t          ],\n622\t          [\n623\t            2,\n624\t            0.73,\n625\t            0.74,\n626\t            -0.01,\n627\t            1.25,\n628\t            22\n629\t          ]\n630\t        ]\n631\t      },\n632\t      {\n633\t        \"name\": \"Leave-One-Judge-Out Residual Diagnostic\",\n634\t        \"columns\": [\n635\t          \"Removed_judge\",\n636\t          \"Family_overlap?\",\n637\t          \"Max_residual_change\",\n638\t          \"Systematic_bias?\"\n639\t        ],\n640\t        \"rows\": [\n641\t          [\n642\t            \"GPT-5.5\",\n643\t            \"Yes (GPT family targets)\",\n644\t            0.04,\n645\t            \"No\"\n646\t          ],\n647\t          [\n648\t            \"Claude Opus 4.7\",\n649\t            \"Yes (Claude targets)\",\n650\t            0.03,\n651\t            \"No\"\n652\t          ],\n653\t          [\n654\t            \"DeepSeek-V4-Pro\",\n655\t            \"Yes (DeepSeek targets)\",\n656\t            0.02,\n657\t            \"No\"\n658\t          ],\n659\t          [\n660\t            \"Qwen3-235B-A22B\",\n661\t            \"Yes (Qwen targets)\",\n662\t            0.03,\n663\t            \"No\"\n664\t          ],\n665\t          [\n666\t            \"Llama 4 Maverick\",\n667\t            \"Yes (Llama targets)\",\n668\t            0.02,\n669\t            \"No\"\n670\t          ]\n671\t        ]\n672\t      }\n673\t    ],\n674\t    \"figures\": [],\n675\t    \"analysis\": \"This experiment confirms G-MFRM adds nothing over MFRM-only at any judge count in the worst tier. The contamination facet is unidentifiable in practice. However, the graceful degradation finding is publishable as a safety property: G-MFRM doesn't HARM rankings when contamination is absent. The leave-one-judge-out diagnostic is useful as a methodological contribution regardless of contamination magnitude — it provides a principled way to detect preference leakage without requiring contamination to be present.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, G-MFRM never outperforms MFRM-only at any judge count because contamination is negligible — the gating mechanism adds parameters without adding signal. At 2 judges, G-MFRM actually slightly underperforms MFRM-only (Δτ=-0.01) due to over-parameterization with insufficient data. Convergence degrades progressively as judges decrease (R̂=1.25 at 2 judges) because c_n parameters are completely unidentifiable with sparse judge coverage and weak e_i. The leave-one-judge-out diagnostic shows no systematic same-family bias pattern — residual changes are small (max 0.04) because contamination effects are too weak to create detectable judge-model interactions. The practical conclusion is that with ≥4 judges, G-MFRM is harmless (degrades gracefully to MFRM level) but never beneficial for ranking.\"\n676\t  },\n677\t  {\n678\t    \"name\": \"exposure_indicator_ablation\",\n679\t    \"description\": \"Ablation: contribution of each exposure source to e_i quality; leave-one-out analysis\",\n680\t    \"results\": {\n681\t      \"full_pipeline_alpha\": 0.24,\n682\t      \"leave_out_performance_gap_alpha\": 0.15,\n683\t      \"leave_out_temporal_alpha\": 0.22,\n684\t      \"leave_out_logprober_alpha\": 0.2,\n685\t      \"performance_gap_unique_contribution\": 0.09,\n686\t      \"temporal_unique_contribution\": 0.02,\n687\t      \"logprober_unique_contribution\": 0.04,\n688\t      \"performance_gap_only_tau\": 0.76,\n689\t      \"temporal_only_tau\": 0.75,\n690\t      \"logprober_only_tau\": 0.75,\n691\t      \"full_pipeline_tau\": 0.77,\n692\t      \"no_ei_mfrm_only_tau\": 0.77,\n693\t      \"delta_any_source_vs_no_ei\": [\n694\t        -0.02,\n695\t        0.01\n696\t      ]\n697\t    },\n698\t    \"tables\": [\n699\t      {\n700\t        \"name\": \"Exposure Source Ablation\",\n701\t        \"columns\": [\n702\t          \"Configuration\",\n703\t          \"Alpha\",\n704\t          \"e_i_mean\",\n705\t          \"e_i_SD\",\n706\t          \"Downstream_tau\",\n707\t          \"Delta_vs_MFRM_only\"\n708\t        ],\n709\t        \"rows\": [\n710\t          [\n711\t            \"Full pipeline (3 sources)\",\n712\t            0.24,\n713\t            0.18,\n714\t            0.22,\n715\t            0.77,\n716\t            0.0\n717\t          ],\n718\t          [\n719\t            \"Without performance gap\",\n720\t            0.15,\n721\t            0.16,\n722\t            0.2,\n723\t            0.76,\n724\t            -0.01\n725\t          ],\n726\t          [\n727\t            \"Without temporal\",\n728\t            0.22,\n729\t            0.17,\n730\t            0.21,\n731\t            0.77,\n732\t            0.0\n733\t          ],\n734\t          [\n735\t            \"Without LogProber\",\n736\t            0.2,\n737\t            0.19,\n738\t            0.23,\n739\t            0.76,\n740\t            -0.01\n741\t          ],\n742\t          [\n743\t            \"Performance gap only\",\n744\t            \"N/A\",\n745\t            0.15,\n746\t            0.19,\n747\t            0.76,\n748\t            -0.01\n749\t          ],\n750\t          [\n751\t            \"No e_i (MFRM-only)\",\n752\t            \"N/A\",\n753\t            \"N/A\",\n754\t            \"N/A\",\n755\t            0.77,\n756\t            \"ref\"\n757\t          ]\n758\t        ]\n759\t      }\n760\t    ],\n761\t    \"figures\": [],\n762\t    \"analysis\": \"The ablation confirms that no e_i source meaningfully contributes to ranking quality under worst tier. This is not a pipeline failure per se — it's that the signal being measured (item exposure) is genuinely near-zero on these benchmarks. Pivot narrative: the pipeline is VALIDATED for measuring exposure (internal structure exists, α=0.24 > 0), but the underlying construct is at floor. This supports the 'benchmarks are clean' narrative contribution.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, e_i pipeline has internal consistency α=0.24 (floor). When we ablate sources, the full pipeline (α=0.24) is already so weak that removing any single source barely changes downstream τ. The performance gap is the strongest single source but still weak (unique contribution = 0.09 to α). Temporal and LogProber add negligible incremental signal. Critically, ALL configurations of e_i produce the same downstream τ (0.75–0.77) because the contamination correction itself is inconsequential — varying e_i quality doesn't matter when c_n ≈ 0. The 'no e_i' condition (MFRM-only, τ=0.77) matches or beats all e_i conditions, confirming the entire exposure estimation pipeline is effort without payoff on these benchmarks.\"\n763\t  },\n764\t  {\n765\t    \"name\": \"facet_fit_diagnostics\",\n766\t    \"description\": \"Measurement quality: infit/outfit, person-fit, divergent transitions, posterior predictive checks\",\n767\t    \"results\": {\n768\t      \"theta_infit_mean\": 1.02,\n769\t      \"theta_outfit_mean\": 1.05,\n770\t      \"theta_misfit_pct\": 4.5,\n771\t      \"b_infit_mean\": 0.99,\n772\t      \"b_outfit_mean\": 1.03,\n773\t      \"b_misfit_pct\": 6.2,\n774\t      \"d_infit_mean\": 1.01,\n775\t      \"d_outfit_mean\": 1.04,\n776\t      \"d_misfit_pct\": 12.5,\n777\t      \"cn_infit_mean\": 1.15,\n778\t      \"cn_outfit_mean\": 1.28,\n779\t      \"cn_misfit_pct\": 36.4,\n780\t      \"divergent_transitions_total\": 142,\n781\t      \"divergent_transitions_pct\": 2.2,\n782\t      \"posterior_predictive_p_global\": 0.41,\n783\t      \"posterior_predictive_p_items_below_0.05_pct\": 8.3,\n784\t      \"lz_stage1_vs_cn_stage3_correlation\": 0.18,\n785\t      \"stage1_emri_flagged_models\": 3,\n786\t      \"cn_flagged_same_models\": 1\n787\t    },\n788\t    \"tables\": [\n789\t      {\n790\t        \"name\": \"Facet Fit Summary\",\n791\t        \"columns\": [\n792\t          \"Facet\",\n793\t          \"N\",\n794\t          \"Infit_mean\",\n795\t          \"Outfit_mean\",\n796\t          \"Misfit_pct\",\n797\t          \"Interpretation\"\n798\t        ],\n799\t        \"rows\": [\n800\t          [\n801\t            \"theta (ability)\",\n802\t            22,\n803\t            1.02,\n804\t            1.05,\n805\t            4.5,\n806\t            \"Good fit\"\n807\t          ],\n808\t          [\n809\t            \"b (difficulty)\",\n810\t            580,\n811\t            0.99,\n812\t            1.03,\n813\t            6.2,\n814\t            \"Good fit\"\n815\t          ],\n816\t          [\n817\t            \"d (severity)\",\n818\t            8,\n819\t            1.01,\n820\t            1.04,\n821\t            12.5,\n822\t            \"Acceptable (1/8 judges borderline)\"\n823\t          ],\n824\t          [\n825\t            \"c_n (contamination)\",\n826\t            22,\n827\t            1.15,\n828\t            1.28,\n829\t            36.4,\n830\t            \"Poor fit — construct at floor\"\n831\t          ]\n832\t        ]\n833\t      },\n834\t      {\n835\t        \"name\": \"Convergence Diagnostics Detail\",\n836\t        \"columns\": [\n837\t          \"Parameter_group\",\n838\t          \"R_hat_median\",\n839\t          \"R_hat_max\",\n840\t          \"ESS_median\",\n841\t          \"ESS_min\",\n842\t          \"Divergent_pct\"\n843\t        ],\n844\t        \"rows\": [\n845\t          [\n846\t            \"theta\",\n847\t            1.01,\n848\t            1.03,\n849\t            892,\n850\t            412,\n851\t            0.1\n852\t          ],\n853\t          [\n854\t            \"b\",\n855\t            1.0,\n856\t            1.02,\n857\t            1105,\n858\t            523,\n859\t            0.2\n860\t          ],\n861\t          [\n862\t            \"d\",\n863\t            1.01,\n864\t            1.02,\n865\t            945,\n866\t            678,\n867\t            0.1\n868\t          ],\n869\t          [\n870\t            \"c_n\",\n871\t            1.05,\n872\t            1.18,\n873\t            156,\n874\t            34,\n875\t            1.8\n876\t          ]\n877\t        ]\n878\t      }\n879\t    ],\n880\t    \"figures\": [],\n881\t    \"analysis\": \"Diagnostics reveal a split personality: G-MFRM as MFRM (θ,b,d) works well; the contamination extension (c_n) is poorly identified and misfitting. This is consistent with worst tier where c_n attempts to model a near-absent signal. The 36.4% c_n misfit rate would normally indicate model failure, but reframed: it shows the contamination term is unnecessary for this data. Pivot value: these diagnostics themselves demonstrate G-MFRM's utility as a diagnostic framework — the misfit detection tells practitioners 'contamination is not a concern on this benchmark,' which is actionable information.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, θ, b, and d facets fit well because MFRM structure is appropriate for multi-judge data (these are just standard psychometric facets). But c_n shows substantial misfit: 36.4% of models have outfit > 1.3, indicating the rank-1 contamination model (c_n·e_i) doesn't describe the residual patterns. This is because when true contamination is near-zero, the c_n parameters are fitting noise rather than signal — any nonzero c_n estimate absorbs heterogeneous residual variance, producing outfit. The lz (person-fit) from Stage 1 correlates only weakly (r=0.18) with c_n from Stage 3, meaning the two stages are identifying different 'aberrant' patterns — further evidence that c_n is not measuring a coherent construct on this data. Divergent transitions (2.2%) cluster around c_n parameters near the half-Cauchy boundary. The model fits well OVERALL (global posterior predictive p=0.41) because c_n terms are near-zero and the model is effectively MFRM.\"\n882\t  },\n883\t  {\n884\t    \"name\": \"confounding_formal_observation\",\n885\t    \"description\": \"Formal OVB bias derivation + Monte Carlo validation across realistic parameter ranges; synthetic c_n recovery\",\n886\t    \"results\": {\n887\t      \"bias_formula_derived\": true,\n888\t      \"monte_carlo_n_conditions\": 48,\n889\t      \"monte_carlo_reps_per_condition\": 100,\n890\t      \"bias_magnitude_at_cn_0.15\": 0.02,\n891\t      \"bias_magnitude_at_cn_0.5\": 0.08,\n892\t      \"bias_magnitude_at_cn_1.5\": 0.31,\n893\t      \"bias_direction_prediction_accuracy\": 0.94,\n894\t      \"cn_recovery_rmse_at_real_magnitude\": 0.42,\n895\t      \"cn_recovery_95ci_coverage_at_real\": 0.78,\n896\t      \"cn_recovery_rmse_at_mvp_magnitude\": 0.18,\n897\t      \"cn_recovery_95ci_coverage_at_mvp\": 0.91,\n898\t      \"confounding_scatter_r_at_real_cn\": 0.12,\n899\t      \"confounding_scatter_r_at_mvp_cn\": 0.65,\n900\t      \"orthogonality_test_real_data\": 0.09\n901\t    },\n902\t    \"tables\": [\n903\t      {\n904\t        \"name\": \"Monte Carlo Bias Magnitude by c_n Level\",\n905\t        \"columns\": [\n906\t          \"c_n_regime\",\n907\t          \"Bias_theta_RMSE\",\n908\t          \"Scatter_r\",\n909\t          \"Sequential_degradation_pct\",\n910\t          \"Practical_significance\"\n911\t        ],\n912\t        \"rows\": [\n913\t          [\n914\t            \"c_n = 0.15 (real)\",\n915\t            0.02,\n916\t            0.12,\n917\t            1.8,\n918\t            \"Negligible\"\n919\t          ],\n920\t          [\n921\t            \"c_n = 0.50 (mvp_001)\",\n922\t            0.08,\n923\t            0.38,\n924\t            12.5,\n925\t            \"Marginal\"\n926\t          ],\n927\t          [\n928\t            \"c_n = 1.00\",\n929\t            0.19,\n930\t            0.52,\n931\t            28.3,\n932\t            \"Moderate\"\n933\t          ],\n934\t          [\n935\t            \"c_n = 1.50 (mvp_002/003)\",\n936\t            0.31,\n937\t            0.65,\n938\t            43.8,\n939\t            \"Large\"\n940\t          ]\n941\t        ]\n942\t      },\n943\t      {\n944\t        \"name\": \"Synthetic c_n Recovery at Real-Data Magnitude\",\n945\t        \"columns\": [\n946\t          \"Metric\",\n947\t          \"c_n=0.15\",\n948\t          \"c_n=0.50\",\n949\t          \"c_n=1.50\"\n950\t        ],\n951\t        \"rows\": [\n952\t          [\n953\t            \"c_n RMSE\",\n954\t            0.42,\n955\t            0.28,\n956\t            0.18\n957\t          ],\n958\t          [\n959\t            \"95% CI coverage\",\n960\t            0.78,\n961\t            0.87,\n962\t            0.91\n963\t          ],\n964\t          [\n965\t            \"c_n correlation (true vs fitted)\",\n966\t            0.35,\n967\t            0.72,\n968\t            0.92\n969\t          ],\n970\t          [\n971\t            \"Pct models correctly ranked by c_n\",\n972\t            0.55,\n973\t            0.78,\n974\t            0.94\n975\t          ]\n976\t        ]\n977\t      }\n978\t    ],\n979\t    \"figures\": [],\n980\t    \"analysis\": \"The formal observation remains a valid theoretical contribution — OVB exists regardless of magnitude. But the practical punchline under worst tier: the bias is negligible (0.02 logits) because contamination is too low to create meaningful confounding. The synthetic c_n recovery at real-data magnitude (correlation 0.35) confirms G-MFRM cannot reliably rank models by contamination susceptibility when the signal is this weak. Pivot framing: 'we derive the confounding mechanism and show it activates under conditions X; current benchmarks happen to NOT meet these conditions; the correction is available when needed.'\\n\\nThe formal OVB observation is valid regardless of contamination magnitude — it's a mathematical identity. Monte Carlo confirms: bias direction prediction is 94% accurate across all conditions. BUT the practical bias magnitude scales linearly with c_n. At real-data contamination levels (c_n≈0.15), the sequential estimator bias is only 0.02 logits — negligible relative to posterior uncertainty. The confounding scatter r drops from 0.65 (mvp_002/003 regime) to 0.12 (real-data regime) because d_j and c_n·e_i are approximately orthogonal when c_n is near-zero. Synthetic c_n recovery shows the method WORKS at high contamination (RMSE=0.18, coverage=0.91 at mvp levels) but FAILS at real-data levels (RMSE=0.42, coverage=0.78) — the posterior is too wide to be informative. This confirms the core assumption: 'd_j and c_n·e_i approximately orthogonal in this dataset' — confounding exists in theory but not in practice.\"\n981\t  },\n982\t  {\n983\t    \"name\": \"wildbench_replication\",\n984\t    \"description\": \"Replicate G-MFRM pipeline on WildBench as third benchmark for cross-benchmark generalization\",\n985\t    \"results\": {\n986\t      \"wildbench_n_items\": 1024,\n987\t      \"wildbench_n_judges\": 6,\n988\t      \"wildbench_kendall_tau_gmfrm\": 0.74,\n989\t      \"wildbench_kendall_tau_mfrm\": 0.75,\n990\t      \"wildbench_kendall_tau_uda\": 0.76,\n991\t      \"wildbench_delta_tau_gmfrm_vs_best\": -0.02,\n992\t      \"wildbench_cn_median\": 0.09,\n993\t      \"wildbench_cn_max\": 0.38,\n994\t      \"wildbench_ei_median\": 0.08,\n995\t      \"wildbench_convergence_rhat_max\": 1.14,\n996\t      \"wildbench_convergence_ess_min\": 41,\n997\t      \"cross_benchmark_cn_correlation\": 0.28,\n998\t      \"cross_benchmark_theta_correlation\": 0.92\n999\t    },\n1000\t    \"tables\": [\n1001\t      {\n1002\t        \"name\": \"Cross-Benchmark Comparison\",\n1003\t        \"columns\": [\n1004\t          \"Benchmark\",\n1005\t          \"tau_GMFRM\",\n1006\t          \"tau_MFRM\",\n1007\t          \"tau_UDA\",\n1008\t          \"cn_median\",\n1009\t          \"ei_median\"\n1010\t        ],\n1011\t        \"rows\": [\n1012\t          [\n1013\t            \"MT-Bench\",\n1014\t            0.76,\n1015\t            0.75,\n1016\t            0.78,\n1017\t            0.17,\n1018\t            0.14\n1019\t          ],\n1020\t          [\n1021\t            \"Arena-Hard\",\n1022\t            0.78,\n1023\t            0.77,\n1024\t            0.79,\n1025\t            0.14,\n1026\t            0.11\n1027\t          ],\n1028\t          [\n1029\t            \"WildBench\",\n1030\t            0.74,\n1031\t            0.75,\n1032\t            0.76,\n1033\t            0.09,\n1034\t            0.08\n1035\t          ]\n1036\t        ]\n1037\t      }\n1038\t    ],\n1039\t    \"figures\": [],\n1040\t    \"analysis\": \"WildBench replication confirms worst-tier pattern generalizes: negligible contamination, no ranking benefit from G-MFRM. The low cross-benchmark c_n correlation (0.28) suggests that contamination — what little exists — is item-specific rather than reflecting stable model-level susceptibility. This weakens the c_n construct validity further but supports the 'benchmarks are clean' narrative.\\n\\nWildBench uses more diverse, harder items sourced from real user interactions — these items are LESS likely to be contaminated than MT-Bench/Arena-Hard because they're more recent and less standardized. Under worst tier, WildBench shows even lower contamination (median c_n=0.09) than the other benchmarks. G-MFRM slightly underperforms both MFRM-only and UDA because the contamination parameters add noise. Cross-benchmark c_n correlation is weak (r=0.28) — models don't show consistent contamination patterns across benchmarks, suggesting what little contamination exists is benchmark-specific (item-level memorization) rather than model-level training data overlap. Cross-benchmark θ correlation (0.92) confirms ability estimates are stable regardless of contamination correction method.\"\n1041\t  },\n1042\t  {\n1043\t    \"name\": \"adversarial_robustness\",\n1044\t    \"description\": \"Test G-MFRM robustness against adversarial contamination following Wang et al. (ICLR 2026)\",\n1045\t    \"results\": {\n1046\t      \"adversarial_injection_levels\": [\n1047\t        0.0,\n1048\t        0.3,\n1049\t        0.5,\n1050\t        1.0,\n1051\t        1.5\n1052\t      ],\n1053\t      \"tau_gmfrm_at_0.0\": 0.77,\n1054\t      \"tau_gmfrm_at_0.3\": 0.76,\n1055\t      \"tau_gmfrm_at_0.5\": 0.78,\n1056\t      \"tau_gmfrm_at_1.0\": 0.82,\n1057\t      \"tau_gmfrm_at_1.5\": 0.87,\n1058\t      \"tau_sequential_at_1.5\": 0.79,\n1059\t      \"tau_mfrm_at_1.5\": 0.75,\n1060\t      \"crossover_point_cn\": 0.7,\n1061\t      \"cn_recovery_at_injection_1.5\": 0.81,\n1062\t      \"detection_power_at_real_cn\": 0.15,\n1063\t      \"detection_power_at_injected_1.5\": 0.92\n1064\t    },\n1065\t    \"tables\": [\n1066\t      {\n1067\t        \"name\": \"Adversarial Injection Results\",\n1068\t        \"columns\": [\n1069\t          \"Injection_level\",\n1070\t          \"tau_GMFRM\",\n1071\t          \"tau_Sequential\",\n1072\t          \"tau_MFRM\",\n1073\t          \"cn_recovery_corr\",\n1074\t          \"Detection_power\"\n1075\t        ],\n1076\t        \"rows\": [\n1077\t          [\n1078\t            0.0,\n1079\t            0.77,\n1080\t            0.76,\n1081\t            0.77,\n1082\t            0.35,\n1083\t            0.15\n1084\t          ],\n1085\t          [\n1086\t            0.3,\n1087\t            0.76,\n1088\t            0.76,\n1089\t            0.76,\n1090\t            0.42,\n1091\t            0.25\n1092\t          ],\n1093\t          [\n1094\t            0.5,\n1095\t            0.78,\n1096\t            0.77,\n1097\t            0.76,\n1098\t            0.58,\n1099\t            0.45\n1100\t          ],\n1101\t          [\n1102\t            1.0,\n1103\t            0.82,\n1104\t            0.79,\n1105\t            0.75,\n1106\t            0.74,\n1107\t            0.78\n1108\t          ],\n1109\t          [\n1110\t            1.5,\n1111\t            0.87,\n1112\t            0.79,\n1113\t            0.75,\n1114\t            0.81,\n1115\t            0.92\n1116\t          ]\n1117\t        ]\n1118\t      }\n1119\t    ],\n1120\t    \"figures\": [],\n1121\t    \"analysis\": \"Adversarial robustness provides the strongest pivot evidence: G-MFRM works when contamination exists, it just doesn't exist on current benchmarks. The crossover at c_n=0.7 gives a principled threshold for when G-MFRM becomes necessary. Framing: 'G-MFRM is an insurance policy — unnecessary today but protective against future contamination increases as training data grows.' Also validates the methodology for historical benchmarks (e.g., MMLU original) where contamination IS known to be high.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, natural contamination is negligible — but we can INJECT synthetic contamination to demonstrate G-MFRM works when contamination IS present. By artificially inflating scores for specific models on high-e_i items, we create a controlled setting. At injection level 1.5 (matching mvp_002/003), G-MFRM recovers well (τ=0.87 vs sequential's 0.79). The crossover point where G-MFRM starts outperforming is c_n≈0.7 — above real-data levels but demonstrating the method's potential. Detection power at real c_n levels is only 0.15 (cannot detect what isn't there), but jumps to 0.92 at injected 1.5. This experiment redeems the method theoretically while confirming it's unnecessary on current benchmarks.\"\n1122\t  }\n1123\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0ac9dd48-cd85-42c4-ac70-70acb66bc713","uuid":"071d6aee-f53c-4498-94c9-c6ca315cb5e6","timestamp":"2026-05-22T04:47:22.059Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-prod43/projects/gated_mfrm_judge_bias/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T041217/worst/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"multi_judge_data_collection\",\n    \"description\": \"Collect multi-judge evaluation data on MT-Bench and Arena-Hard with 8 judges × 20+ target models\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"total_judges_collected\": 8,\n      \"total_target_models\": 22,\n      \"total_judgments_mtbench\": 14080,\n      \"total_judgments_arenahard\": 35200,\n      \"position_swap_agreement\": 0.72,\n      \"inter_judge_agreement_fleiss_kappa\": 0.38,\n      \"judge_severity_range_raw\": 0.85,\n      \"missing_data_pct\": 3.2,\n      \"api_cost_usd\": 870,\n      \"gpu_hours_used\": 118\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Judge Collection Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Judge\",\n          \"N_judgments\",\n          \"Mean_score\",\n          \"SD\",\n          \"Severity_range\",\n          \"Position_bias\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"GPT-5.5\",\n            4400,\n            7.82,\n            1.21,\n            \"ref\",\n            0.08\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Claude Opus 4.7\",\n            4400,\n            7.45,\n            1.35,\n            -0.37,\n            0.12\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Claude Sonnet 4.6\",\n            4400,\n            7.61,\n            1.18,\n            -0.21,\n            0.09\n          ],\n          [\n            \"DeepSeek-V4-Pro\",\n            4400,\n            7.28,\n            1.42,\n            -0.54,\n            0.15\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-235B-A22B\",\n            4400,\n            7.53,\n            1.08,\n            -0.29,\n            0.11\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3.6-27B\",\n            4400,\n            7.91,\n            0.82,\n            0.09,\n            0.06\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Llama 4 Maverick\",\n            4400,\n            7.15,\n            1.48,\n            -0.67,\n            0.14\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Gemini 2.5 Pro\",\n            4400,\n            7.68,\n            1.25,\n            -0.14,\n            0.1\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Data quality is adequate for MFRM fitting but the score distributions already hint at worst-tier outcome: no judge shows dramatically different severity for known-contaminated vs clean models. The compressed scoring range of Qwen3.6-27B (SD=0.82) will limit its contribution to facet separation.\\n\\nData collection itself is scenario-independent — the judges produce scores regardless of whether contamination exists. However, under worst tier the inter-judge agreement is moderate (κ≈0.38) because judges disagree primarily on difficulty/style rather than contamination-driven inflation. Position swap agreement is typical (0.72) indicating normal positional bias. The raw judge severity range (0.85 logits) is present but moderate — some judges (e.g., Qwen3.6-27B) show compressed scoring ranges (<0.3 logit range), limiting MFRM's value for those facets.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"item_exposure_estimation\",\n    \"description\": \"Estimate per-item exposure e_i via three-source pipeline (performance gap, temporal, LogProber)\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"ei_mean\": 0.18,\n      \"ei_median\": 0.12,\n      \"ei_std\": 0.22,\n      \"ei_max\": 0.71,\n      \"ei_iqr\": [\n        0.04,\n        0.28\n      ],\n      \"pct_items_ei_above_0.5\": 8.5,\n      \"performance_gap_signal_snr\": 1.2,\n      \"temporal_signal_coverage_pct\": 35,\n      \"logprober_coverage_pct\": 45,\n      \"source_intercorrelation_gap_temporal\": 0.28,\n      \"source_intercorrelation_gap_logprober\": 0.31,\n      \"source_intercorrelation_temporal_logprober\": 0.19,\n      \"weighted_composite_internal_consistency_alpha\": 0.24,\n      \"spline_correction_r2\": 0.42,\n      \"reference_variant_quality_check_pass_pct\": 78\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Exposure Source Contribution\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Source\",\n          \"Coverage_pct\",\n          \"Mean_signal\",\n          \"SD\",\n          \"SNR\",\n          \"Correlation_with_composite\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Performance gap (primary)\",\n            100,\n            0.15,\n            0.19,\n            1.2,\n            0.78\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Temporal signal\",\n            35,\n            0.21,\n            0.31,\n            0.8,\n            0.52\n          ],\n          [\n            \"LogProber\",\n            45,\n            0.14,\n            0.24,\n            0.9,\n            0.61\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"e_i Distribution by Benchmark\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"N_items\",\n          \"Median_ei\",\n          \"Mean_ei\",\n          \"Pct_above_0.5\",\n          \"Max_ei\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MT-Bench\",\n            80,\n            0.14,\n            0.2,\n            10.0,\n            0.71\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Arena-Hard\",\n            500,\n            0.11,\n            0.17,\n            7.8,\n            0.63\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The e_i pipeline's internal consistency (α=0.24) is a critical weakness. The signal is real but noisy — identifiable in aggregate but unreliable at per-item level. This means Stage 3 fitting will have limited power to separate c_n from noise in e_i. The low median (0.12) confirms that most benchmark items are effectively clean, leaving little contamination signal for G-MFRM to exploit.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, e_i pipeline produces weak/noisy signal (effective σ_e ≈ 0.3–0.5 in the core assumption). Most items show near-zero exposure because current benchmarks (MT-Bench 2024 refresh, Arena-Hard v2) were designed to resist memorization. The three sources have low intercorrelation (0.19–0.31) indicating they capture different noise rather than converging on a true signal. Internal consistency α=0.24 is far below psychometric acceptability (0.70). The few items with e_i > 0.5 are likely legacy items from MT-Bench v1 that survived into the refresh. LogProber coverage is limited (45%) because many target models lack logprob APIs. The spline difficulty correction only explains 42% of variance in raw performance gaps — most of the gap is difficulty-driven, not contamination-driven.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"gmfrm_real_data_fit\",\n    \"description\": \"Fit G-MFRM three-stage pipeline on real data; compare fair scores with Chatbot Arena (Kendall-τ)\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"stage1_mfrm_convergence_rhat_max\": 1.02,\n      \"stage1_mfrm_ess_min\": 312,\n      \"stage3_joint_convergence_rhat_max\": 1.12,\n      \"stage3_joint_ess_min\": 58,\n      \"stage3_cn_rhat_max\": 1.18,\n      \"stage3_cn_ess_min\": 34,\n      \"kendall_tau_arena_gmfrm_mtbench\": 0.76,\n      \"kendall_tau_arena_gmfrm_arenahard\": 0.78,\n      \"kendall_tau_arena_mfrm_only_mtbench\": 0.75,\n      \"kendall_tau_arena_mfrm_only_arenahard\": 0.77,\n      \"cn_median_fitted\": 0.15,\n      \"cn_mean_fitted\": 0.22,\n      \"cn_max_fitted\": 0.61,\n      \"cn_95ci_width_median\": 0.48,\n      \"theta_fair_vs_theta_mfrm_rank_correlation\": 0.97,\n      \"pct_models_rank_change_geq_2\": 9.1,\n      \"divergent_transitions_pct\": 2.8,\n      \"runtime_hours\": 8.5\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"G-MFRM Fit Summary by Benchmark\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"R_hat_max\",\n          \"ESS_min\",\n          \"Divergent_pct\",\n          \"cn_median\",\n          \"cn_max\",\n          \"tau_Arena\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MT-Bench\",\n            1.12,\n            58,\n            2.8,\n            0.17,\n            0.61,\n            0.76\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Arena-Hard\",\n            1.08,\n            72,\n            1.5,\n            0.14,\n            0.53,\n            0.78\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Fitted c_n Distribution (Top 5 Models)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"c_n_mean\",\n          \"c_n_95CI_low\",\n          \"c_n_95CI_high\",\n          \"CI_width\",\n          \"R_hat\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Model_A (suspected legacy)\",\n            0.61,\n            0.18,\n            1.12,\n            0.94,\n            1.15\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Model_B\",\n            0.43,\n            0.08,\n            0.89,\n            0.81,\n            1.18\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Model_C\",\n            0.35,\n            0.05,\n            0.74,\n            0.69,\n            1.11\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Model_D\",\n            0.22,\n            0.01,\n            0.58,\n            0.57,\n            1.09\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Model_E\",\n            0.19,\n            0.01,\n            0.52,\n            0.51,\n            1.07\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Stage 1 MFRM fits well (R̂=1.02) — judge severity and item difficulty are well-identified. But Stage 3 struggles with c_n: the combination of weak e_i signal and low true contamination means the data cannot distinguish c_n=0 from c_n=0.3 for most models. The 2.8% divergent transitions on MT-Bench indicate the posterior geometry is problematic near the c_n≈0 boundary. Practically, fair scores θ̂ are indistinguishable from MFRM-only estimates for 20/22 models. The G-MFRM 'correction' affects only 2-3 models with possible legacy contamination, and even those corrections are within posterior uncertainty.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, real c_n distribution has median < 0.3 (fitted median=0.15). Most models have near-zero contamination susceptibility — the c_n posterior piles up near the half-Cauchy prior's mode. The few models with c_n > 0.4 have wide credible intervals (median CI width=0.48) because e_i signal is weak. Stage 3 convergence is problematic: R̂=1.12 overall and up to 1.18 for c_n parameters specifically, with ESS as low as 34 for some c_n. This is because the data provides minimal information about c_n when e_i is near-zero for most items — the likelihood surface is nearly flat in the c_n direction. The resulting fair scores θ̂ barely differ from MFRM-only θ̂ (rank correlation 0.97), meaning the contamination correction has negligible practical effect. Kendall-τ with Arena is 0.76–0.78, within the expected range for this tier but not meaningfully better than MFRM-only.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"baseline_comparison\",\n    \"description\": \"Compare G-MFRM against 8 baselines; Kendall-τ and Spearman-ρ with Chatbot Arena; rank change analysis\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"kendall_tau_raw_average\": 0.74,\n      \"kendall_tau_mfrm_only\": 0.77,\n      \"kendall_tau_sequential\": 0.76,\n      \"kendall_tau_cyclicjudge\": 0.78,\n      \"kendall_tau_cyclicjudge_discard\": 0.77,\n      \"kendall_tau_dcr\": 0.75,\n      \"kendall_tau_uda\": 0.79,\n      \"kendall_tau_prefleak_calib\": 0.76,\n      \"kendall_tau_gmfrm\": 0.77,\n      \"best_baseline\": \"UDA\",\n      \"delta_tau_vs_best_baseline\": -0.02,\n      \"delta_tau_vs_mfrm_only\": 0.0,\n      \"wilcoxon_p_gmfrm_vs_uda\": 0.34,\n      \"wilcoxon_p_gmfrm_vs_cyclicjudge\": 0.62,\n      \"spearman_rho_gmfrm\": 0.89,\n      \"spearman_rho_best_baseline\": 0.91,\n      \"rank_change_models_geq2_positions\": 2,\n      \"top5_stability_gmfrm\": 0.8,\n      \"top5_stability_best_baseline\": 0.8,\n      \"post_hoc_power_for_observed_delta\": 0.12,\n      \"n_models\": 22\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Main Comparison Table\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"Kendall_tau\",\n          \"Spearman_rho\",\n          \"Rank_changes_geq2\",\n          \"Top5_stable\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Raw multi-judge average\",\n            0.74,\n            0.86,\n            \"ref\",\n            \"ref\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MFRM-only (Stage 1)\",\n            0.77,\n            0.89,\n            3,\n            \"4/5\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Sequential (MFRM→contamination)\",\n            0.76,\n            0.88,\n            2,\n            \"4/5\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CyclicJudge\",\n            0.78,\n            0.9,\n            3,\n            \"4/5\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CyclicJudge + item discard\",\n            0.77,\n            0.89,\n            2,\n            \"4/5\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"DCR Factor\",\n            0.75,\n            0.87,\n            2,\n            \"4/5\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"UDA\",\n            0.79,\n            0.91,\n            4,\n            \"4/5\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Preference Leakage calibration\",\n            0.76,\n            0.88,\n            2,\n            \"4/5\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"G-MFRM (ours)\",\n            0.77,\n            0.89,\n            2,\n            \"4/5\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Statistical Tests\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Comparison\",\n          \"Delta_tau\",\n          \"Wilcoxon_p\",\n          \"Power_at_alpha_0.05\",\n          \"Significant?\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"G-MFRM vs UDA\",\n            -0.02,\n            0.34,\n            0.12,\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"G-MFRM vs CyclicJudge\",\n            -0.01,\n            0.62,\n            0.08,\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"G-MFRM vs MFRM-only\",\n            0.0,\n            0.91,\n            0.05,\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"G-MFRM vs Sequential\",\n            0.01,\n            0.55,\n            0.09,\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"G-MFRM vs Raw average\",\n            0.03,\n            0.18,\n            0.22,\n            \"No\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Claim 3 is dead. G-MFRM does not improve over best baselines — it slightly underperforms UDA. Post-hoc power analysis (power=0.12) confirms the study is fundamentally underpowered to detect the observed effect size with N=22 models. Even with 50 models, power would be ~0.25 for Δτ=0.02. The ranking improvement is non-significant by any standard. MFRM-only and G-MFRM produce identical τ, confirming the contamination gating mechanism adds no signal. Pivot narrative: G-MFRM 'degrades gracefully' to MFRM level when contamination is absent — this is a feature, not a failure.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, Δτ is in [-0.02, 0.01] range. G-MFRM (τ=0.77) is actually slightly BELOW UDA (τ=0.79) — UDA's neural K-factor adjustment captures judge-specific biases that are the dominant source of ranking noise when contamination is negligible. CyclicJudge (τ=0.78) also edges out G-MFRM because round-robin bias elimination is effective when contamination is near-zero. The Wilcoxon test is massively underpowered (power=0.12 at N=22 models for detecting Δτ=0.02) — we cannot distinguish methods statistically. G-MFRM ties MFRM-only (both τ=0.77), confirming the contamination correction adds nothing when there's nothing to correct. Rank changes are minimal: only 2 models shift ≥2 positions between G-MFRM and best baseline, and these are within posterior uncertainty. This is the fatal result for Claim 3.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"contamination_magnitude_calibration\",\n    \"description\": \"Estimate real contamination magnitude; determine if c_n matches mvp_001 (weak) or mvp_002 (strong)\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"real_cn_median\": 0.15,\n      \"real_cn_mean\": 0.22,\n      \"real_cn_max\": 0.61,\n      \"real_cn_iqr\": [\n        0.04,\n        0.32\n      ],\n      \"pct_models_cn_above_0.5\": 9.1,\n      \"pct_models_cn_above_1.0\": 0.0,\n      \"closest_mvp_match\": \"below mvp_001\",\n      \"mvp_001_cn_mean_was\": 0.5,\n      \"ratio_real_to_mvp001\": 0.44,\n      \"constat_delta_correlation_with_cn\": 0.32,\n      \"constat_delta_median\": 0.08,\n      \"cn_distribution_shape\": \"heavily right-skewed, mode near 0\",\n      \"bimodality_coefficient\": 0.31\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Contamination Magnitude Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Source\",\n          \"cn_median\",\n          \"cn_mean\",\n          \"cn_max\",\n          \"Interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MVP synthetic (mvp_001)\",\n            0.5,\n            0.5,\n            1.0,\n            \"Assumed moderate\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MVP synthetic (mvp_002/003)\",\n            1.5,\n            1.5,\n            2.5,\n            \"Assumed strong\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Real data (this experiment)\",\n            0.15,\n            0.22,\n            0.61,\n            \"Negligible to weak\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Per-Model c_n Estimates\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"c_n_posterior_mean\",\n          \"95CI\",\n          \"ConStat_delta\",\n          \"Category\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"GPT-5.5\",\n            0.08,\n            \"[0.01, 0.29]\",\n            0.05,\n            \"negligible\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Claude Opus 4.7\",\n            0.05,\n            \"[0.00, 0.21]\",\n            0.03,\n            \"negligible\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Llama 4 405B\",\n            0.35,\n            \"[0.06, 0.72]\",\n            0.15,\n            \"weak\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-235B\",\n            0.43,\n            \"[0.08, 0.89]\",\n            0.18,\n            \"weak-moderate\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"DeepSeek-V4\",\n            0.61,\n            \"[0.18, 1.12]\",\n            0.22,\n            \"moderate (max)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This is the definitive worst-tier signal. Real contamination is far below synthetic assumptions. The mvp_002/003 success (43.8% RMSE reduction) required c_n~1.5 — real data has c_n~0.15 (10× weaker). Even mvp_001 assumed c~0.5. The G-MFRM's gating mechanism is solving a problem that barely exists on these benchmarks. Pivot implications: (1) the null finding about contamination magnitude is itself publishable — benchmarks may be cleaner than assumed; (2) G-MFRM's value is as a diagnostic tool that CONFIRMS low contamination rather than correcting for high contamination; (3) need to find or construct a benchmark with known high contamination for validation case.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, real c_n median is in [0.05, 0.3] — we get 0.15, which is BELOW even mvp_001's assumption of c~0.5. The distribution is not bimodal but heavily right-skewed with mode near zero: most models have negligible contamination, 2-3 have moderate (0.4-0.6), none reach 1.0. This is well below the regime where G-MFRM's gating mechanism has meaningful leverage. The ConStat per-model delta validates only weakly (r=0.32) because both signals are near-floor — correlation is attenuated by floor effects and measurement noise. The finding itself IS informative: current benchmarks (MT-Bench 2024 refresh, Arena-Hard v2) appear to have effectively low contamination. This challenges assumptions in contamination literature that training data overlap produces large performance inflation on these specific benchmarks.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"judge_count_sensitivity\",\n    \"description\": \"Vary judges (2,4,6,8); measure G-MFRM degradation; leave-one-judge-out residual diagnostic\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"tau_8_judges\": 0.77,\n      \"tau_6_judges\": 0.76,\n      \"tau_4_judges\": 0.75,\n      \"tau_2_judges\": 0.73,\n      \"tau_mfrm_only_8_judges\": 0.77,\n      \"tau_mfrm_only_4_judges\": 0.75,\n      \"delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_8j\": 0.0,\n      \"delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_6j\": 0.0,\n      \"delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_4j\": 0.0,\n      \"delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_2j\": -0.01,\n      \"cn_identifiability_threshold_judges\": \"not reached\",\n      \"leave_one_out_max_residual_change\": 0.04,\n      \"leave_one_out_same_family_bias_detected\": false,\n      \"convergence_2j_rhat_max\": 1.25,\n      \"convergence_4j_rhat_max\": 1.15,\n      \"convergence_6j_rhat_max\": 1.1\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Judge Count Sensitivity\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"N_judges\",\n          \"tau_GMFRM\",\n          \"tau_MFRM\",\n          \"Delta\",\n          \"R_hat_max\",\n          \"ESS_min_cn\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            8,\n            0.77,\n            0.77,\n            0.0,\n            1.12,\n            58\n          ],\n          [\n            6,\n            0.76,\n            0.76,\n            0.0,\n            1.1,\n            65\n          ],\n          [\n            4,\n            0.75,\n            0.75,\n            0.0,\n            1.15,\n            42\n          ],\n          [\n            2,\n            0.73,\n            0.74,\n            -0.01,\n            1.25,\n            22\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Leave-One-Judge-Out Residual Diagnostic\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Removed_judge\",\n          \"Family_overlap?\",\n          \"Max_residual_change\",\n          \"Systematic_bias?\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"GPT-5.5\",\n            \"Yes (GPT family targets)\",\n            0.04,\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Claude Opus 4.7\",\n            \"Yes (Claude targets)\",\n            0.03,\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"DeepSeek-V4-Pro\",\n            \"Yes (DeepSeek targets)\",\n            0.02,\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-235B-A22B\",\n            \"Yes (Qwen targets)\",\n            0.03,\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Llama 4 Maverick\",\n            \"Yes (Llama targets)\",\n            0.02,\n            \"No\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This experiment confirms G-MFRM adds nothing over MFRM-only at any judge count in the worst tier. The contamination facet is unidentifiable in practice. However, the graceful degradation finding is publishable as a safety property: G-MFRM doesn't HARM rankings when contamination is absent. The leave-one-judge-out diagnostic is useful as a methodological contribution regardless of contamination magnitude — it provides a principled way to detect preference leakage without requiring contamination to be present.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, G-MFRM never outperforms MFRM-only at any judge count because contamination is negligible — the gating mechanism adds parameters without adding signal. At 2 judges, G-MFRM actually slightly underperforms MFRM-only (Δτ=-0.01) due to over-parameterization with insufficient data. Convergence degrades progressively as judges decrease (R̂=1.25 at 2 judges) because c_n parameters are completely unidentifiable with sparse judge coverage and weak e_i. The leave-one-judge-out diagnostic shows no systematic same-family bias pattern — residual changes are small (max 0.04) because contamination effects are too weak to create detectable judge-model interactions. The practical conclusion is that with ≥4 judges, G-MFRM is harmless (degrades gracefully to MFRM level) but never beneficial for ranking.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exposure_indicator_ablation\",\n    \"description\": \"Ablation: contribution of each exposure source to e_i quality; leave-one-out analysis\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"full_pipeline_alpha\": 0.24,\n      \"leave_out_performance_gap_alpha\": 0.15,\n      \"leave_out_temporal_alpha\": 0.22,\n      \"leave_out_logprober_alpha\": 0.2,\n      \"performance_gap_unique_contribution\": 0.09,\n      \"temporal_unique_contribution\": 0.02,\n      \"logprober_unique_contribution\": 0.04,\n      \"performance_gap_only_tau\": 0.76,\n      \"temporal_only_tau\": 0.75,\n      \"logprober_only_tau\": 0.75,\n      \"full_pipeline_tau\": 0.77,\n      \"no_ei_mfrm_only_tau\": 0.77,\n      \"delta_any_source_vs_no_ei\": [\n        -0.02,\n        0.01\n      ]\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Exposure Source Ablation\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Configuration\",\n          \"Alpha\",\n          \"e_i_mean\",\n          \"e_i_SD\",\n          \"Downstream_tau\",\n          \"Delta_vs_MFRM_only\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Full pipeline (3 sources)\",\n            0.24,\n            0.18,\n            0.22,\n            0.77,\n            0.0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Without performance gap\",\n            0.15,\n            0.16,\n            0.2,\n            0.76,\n            -0.01\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Without temporal\",\n            0.22,\n            0.17,\n            0.21,\n            0.77,\n            0.0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Without LogProber\",\n            0.2,\n            0.19,\n            0.23,\n            0.76,\n            -0.01\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Performance gap only\",\n            \"N/A\",\n            0.15,\n            0.19,\n            0.76,\n            -0.01\n          ],\n          [\n            \"No e_i (MFRM-only)\",\n            \"N/A\",\n            \"N/A\",\n            \"N/A\",\n            0.77,\n            \"ref\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The ablation confirms that no e_i source meaningfully contributes to ranking quality under worst tier. This is not a pipeline failure per se — it's that the signal being measured (item exposure) is genuinely near-zero on these benchmarks. Pivot narrative: the pipeline is VALIDATED for measuring exposure (internal structure exists, α=0.24 > 0), but the underlying construct is at floor. This supports the 'benchmarks are clean' narrative contribution.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, e_i pipeline has internal consistency α=0.24 (floor). When we ablate sources, the full pipeline (α=0.24) is already so weak that removing any single source barely changes downstream τ. The performance gap is the strongest single source but still weak (unique contribution = 0.09 to α). Temporal and LogProber add negligible incremental signal. Critically, ALL configurations of e_i produce the same downstream τ (0.75–0.77) because the contamination correction itself is inconsequential — varying e_i quality doesn't matter when c_n ≈ 0. The 'no e_i' condition (MFRM-only, τ=0.77) matches or beats all e_i conditions, confirming the entire exposure estimation pipeline is effort without payoff on these benchmarks.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"facet_fit_diagnostics\",\n    \"description\": \"Measurement quality: infit/outfit, person-fit, divergent transitions, posterior predictive checks\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"theta_infit_mean\": 1.02,\n      \"theta_outfit_mean\": 1.05,\n      \"theta_misfit_pct\": 4.5,\n      \"b_infit_mean\": 0.99,\n      \"b_outfit_mean\": 1.03,\n      \"b_misfit_pct\": 6.2,\n      \"d_infit_mean\": 1.01,\n      \"d_outfit_mean\": 1.04,\n      \"d_misfit_pct\": 12.5,\n      \"cn_infit_mean\": 1.15,\n      \"cn_outfit_mean\": 1.28,\n      \"cn_misfit_pct\": 36.4,\n      \"divergent_transitions_total\": 142,\n      \"divergent_transitions_pct\": 2.2,\n      \"posterior_predictive_p_global\": 0.41,\n      \"posterior_predictive_p_items_below_0.05_pct\": 8.3,\n      \"lz_stage1_vs_cn_stage3_correlation\": 0.18,\n      \"stage1_emri_flagged_models\": 3,\n      \"cn_flagged_same_models\": 1\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Facet Fit Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Facet\",\n          \"N\",\n          \"Infit_mean\",\n          \"Outfit_mean\",\n          \"Misfit_pct\",\n          \"Interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"theta (ability)\",\n            22,\n            1.02,\n            1.05,\n            4.5,\n            \"Good fit\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"b (difficulty)\",\n            580,\n            0.99,\n            1.03,\n            6.2,\n            \"Good fit\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"d (severity)\",\n            8,\n            1.01,\n            1.04,\n            12.5,\n            \"Acceptable (1/8 judges borderline)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"c_n (contamination)\",\n            22,\n            1.15,\n            1.28,\n            36.4,\n            \"Poor fit — construct at floor\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Convergence Diagnostics Detail\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Parameter_group\",\n          \"R_hat_median\",\n          \"R_hat_max\",\n          \"ESS_median\",\n          \"ESS_min\",\n          \"Divergent_pct\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"theta\",\n            1.01,\n            1.03,\n            892,\n            412,\n            0.1\n          ],\n          [\n            \"b\",\n            1.0,\n            1.02,\n            1105,\n            523,\n            0.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"d\",\n            1.01,\n            1.02,\n            945,\n            678,\n            0.1\n          ],\n          [\n            \"c_n\",\n            1.05,\n            1.18,\n            156,\n            34,\n            1.8\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Diagnostics reveal a split personality: G-MFRM as MFRM (θ,b,d) works well; the contamination extension (c_n) is poorly identified and misfitting. This is consistent with worst tier where c_n attempts to model a near-absent signal. The 36.4% c_n misfit rate would normally indicate model failure, but reframed: it shows the contamination term is unnecessary for this data. Pivot value: these diagnostics themselves demonstrate G-MFRM's utility as a diagnostic framework — the misfit detection tells practitioners 'contamination is not a concern on this benchmark,' which is actionable information.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, θ, b, and d facets fit well because MFRM structure is appropriate for multi-judge data (these are just standard psychometric facets). But c_n shows substantial misfit: 36.4% of models have outfit > 1.3, indicating the rank-1 contamination model (c_n·e_i) doesn't describe the residual patterns. This is because when true contamination is near-zero, the c_n parameters are fitting noise rather than signal — any nonzero c_n estimate absorbs heterogeneous residual variance, producing outfit. The lz (person-fit) from Stage 1 correlates only weakly (r=0.18) with c_n from Stage 3, meaning the two stages are identifying different 'aberrant' patterns — further evidence that c_n is not measuring a coherent construct on this data. Divergent transitions (2.2%) cluster around c_n parameters near the half-Cauchy boundary. The model fits well OVERALL (global posterior predictive p=0.41) because c_n terms are near-zero and the model is effectively MFRM.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"confounding_formal_observation\",\n    \"description\": \"Formal OVB bias derivation + Monte Carlo validation across realistic parameter ranges; synthetic c_n recovery\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"bias_formula_derived\": true,\n      \"monte_carlo_n_conditions\": 48,\n      \"monte_carlo_reps_per_condition\": 100,\n      \"bias_magnitude_at_cn_0.15\": 0.02,\n      \"bias_magnitude_at_cn_0.5\": 0.08,\n      \"bias_magnitude_at_cn_1.5\": 0.31,\n      \"bias_direction_prediction_accuracy\": 0.94,\n      \"cn_recovery_rmse_at_real_magnitude\": 0.42,\n      \"cn_recovery_95ci_coverage_at_real\": 0.78,\n      \"cn_recovery_rmse_at_mvp_magnitude\": 0.18,\n      \"cn_recovery_95ci_coverage_at_mvp\": 0.91,\n      \"confounding_scatter_r_at_real_cn\": 0.12,\n      \"confounding_scatter_r_at_mvp_cn\": 0.65,\n      \"orthogonality_test_real_data\": 0.09\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Monte Carlo Bias Magnitude by c_n Level\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"c_n_regime\",\n          \"Bias_theta_RMSE\",\n          \"Scatter_r\",\n          \"Sequential_degradation_pct\",\n          \"Practical_significance\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"c_n = 0.15 (real)\",\n            0.02,\n            0.12,\n            1.8,\n            \"Negligible\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"c_n = 0.50 (mvp_001)\",\n            0.08,\n            0.38,\n            12.5,\n            \"Marginal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"c_n = 1.00\",\n            0.19,\n            0.52,\n            28.3,\n            \"Moderate\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"c_n = 1.50 (mvp_002/003)\",\n            0.31,\n            0.65,\n            43.8,\n            \"Large\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Synthetic c_n Recovery at Real-Data Magnitude\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"c_n=0.15\",\n          \"c_n=0.50\",\n          \"c_n=1.50\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"c_n RMSE\",\n            0.42,\n            0.28,\n            0.18\n          ],\n          [\n            \"95% CI coverage\",\n            0.78,\n            0.87,\n            0.91\n          ],\n          [\n            \"c_n correlation (true vs fitted)\",\n            0.35,\n            0.72,\n            0.92\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Pct models correctly ranked by c_n\",\n            0.55,\n            0.78,\n            0.94\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The formal observation remains a valid theoretical contribution — OVB exists regardless of magnitude. But the practical punchline under worst tier: the bias is negligible (0.02 logits) because contamination is too low to create meaningful confounding. The synthetic c_n recovery at real-data magnitude (correlation 0.35) confirms G-MFRM cannot reliably rank models by contamination susceptibility when the signal is this weak. Pivot framing: 'we derive the confounding mechanism and show it activates under conditions X; current benchmarks happen to NOT meet these conditions; the correction is available when needed.'\\n\\nThe formal OVB observation is valid regardless of contamination magnitude — it's a mathematical identity. Monte Carlo confirms: bias direction prediction is 94% accurate across all conditions. BUT the practical bias magnitude scales linearly with c_n. At real-data contamination levels (c_n≈0.15), the sequential estimator bias is only 0.02 logits — negligible relative to posterior uncertainty. The confounding scatter r drops from 0.65 (mvp_002/003 regime) to 0.12 (real-data regime) because d_j and c_n·e_i are approximately orthogonal when c_n is near-zero. Synthetic c_n recovery shows the method WORKS at high contamination (RMSE=0.18, coverage=0.91 at mvp levels) but FAILS at real-data levels (RMSE=0.42, coverage=0.78) — the posterior is too wide to be informative. This confirms the core assumption: 'd_j and c_n·e_i approximately orthogonal in this dataset' — confounding exists in theory but not in practice.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"wildbench_replication\",\n    \"description\": \"Replicate G-MFRM pipeline on WildBench as third benchmark for cross-benchmark generalization\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"wildbench_n_items\": 1024,\n      \"wildbench_n_judges\": 6,\n      \"wildbench_kendall_tau_gmfrm\": 0.74,\n      \"wildbench_kendall_tau_mfrm\": 0.75,\n      \"wildbench_kendall_tau_uda\": 0.76,\n      \"wildbench_delta_tau_gmfrm_vs_best\": -0.02,\n      \"wildbench_cn_median\": 0.09,\n      \"wildbench_cn_max\": 0.38,\n      \"wildbench_ei_median\": 0.08,\n      \"wildbench_convergence_rhat_max\": 1.14,\n      \"wildbench_convergence_ess_min\": 41,\n      \"cross_benchmark_cn_correlation\": 0.28,\n      \"cross_benchmark_theta_correlation\": 0.92\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Cross-Benchmark Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"tau_GMFRM\",\n          \"tau_MFRM\",\n          \"tau_UDA\",\n          \"cn_median\",\n          \"ei_median\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MT-Bench\",\n            0.76,\n            0.75,\n            0.78,\n            0.17,\n            0.14\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Arena-Hard\",\n            0.78,\n            0.77,\n            0.79,\n            0.14,\n            0.11\n          ],\n          [\n            \"WildBench\",\n            0.74,\n            0.75,\n            0.76,\n            0.09,\n            0.08\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"WildBench replication confirms worst-tier pattern generalizes: negligible contamination, no ranking benefit from G-MFRM. The low cross-benchmark c_n correlation (0.28) suggests that contamination — what little exists — is item-specific rather than reflecting stable model-level susceptibility. This weakens the c_n construct validity further but supports the 'benchmarks are clean' narrative.\\n\\nWildBench uses more diverse, harder items sourced from real user interactions — these items are LESS likely to be contaminated than MT-Bench/Arena-Hard because they're more recent and less standardized. Under worst tier, WildBench shows even lower contamination (median c_n=0.09) than the other benchmarks. G-MFRM slightly underperforms both MFRM-only and UDA because the contamination parameters add noise. Cross-benchmark c_n correlation is weak (r=0.28) — models don't show consistent contamination patterns across benchmarks, suggesting what little contamination exists is benchmark-specific (item-level memorization) rather than model-level training data overlap. Cross-benchmark θ correlation (0.92) confirms ability estimates are stable regardless of contamination correction method.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"adversarial_robustness\",\n    \"description\": \"Test G-MFRM robustness against adversarial contamination following Wang et al. (ICLR 2026)\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"adversarial_injection_levels\": [\n        0.0,\n        0.3,\n        0.5,\n        1.0,\n        1.5\n      ],\n      \"tau_gmfrm_at_0.0\": 0.77,\n      \"tau_gmfrm_at_0.3\": 0.76,\n      \"tau_gmfrm_at_0.5\": 0.78,\n      \"tau_gmfrm_at_1.0\": 0.82,\n      \"tau_gmfrm_at_1.5\": 0.87,\n      \"tau_sequential_at_1.5\": 0.79,\n      \"tau_mfrm_at_1.5\": 0.75,\n      \"crossover_point_cn\": 0.7,\n      \"cn_recovery_at_injection_1.5\": 0.81,\n      \"detection_power_at_real_cn\": 0.15,\n      \"detection_power_at_injected_1.5\": 0.92\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Adversarial Injection Results\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Injection_level\",\n          \"tau_GMFRM\",\n          \"tau_Sequential\",\n          \"tau_MFRM\",\n          \"cn_recovery_corr\",\n          \"Detection_power\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            0.0,\n            0.77,\n            0.76,\n            0.77,\n            0.35,\n            0.15\n          ],\n          [\n            0.3,\n            0.76,\n            0.76,\n            0.76,\n            0.42,\n            0.25\n          ],\n          [\n            0.5,\n            0.78,\n            0.77,\n            0.76,\n            0.58,\n            0.45\n          ],\n          [\n            1.0,\n            0.82,\n            0.79,\n            0.75,\n            0.74,\n            0.78\n          ],\n          [\n            1.5,\n            0.87,\n            0.79,\n            0.75,\n            0.81,\n            0.92\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Adversarial robustness provides the strongest pivot evidence: G-MFRM works when contamination exists, it just doesn't exist on current benchmarks. The crossover at c_n=0.7 gives a principled threshold for when G-MFRM becomes necessary. Framing: 'G-MFRM is an insurance policy — unnecessary today but protective against future contamination increases as training data grows.' Also validates the methodology for historical benchmarks (e.g., MMLU original) where contamination IS known to be high.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, natural contamination is negligible — but we can INJECT synthetic contamination to demonstrate G-MFRM works when contamination IS present. By artificially inflating scores for specific models on high-e_i items, we create a controlled setting. At injection level 1.5 (matching mvp_002/003), G-MFRM recovers well (τ=0.87 vs sequential's 0.79). The crossover point where G-MFRM starts outperforming is c_n≈0.7 — above real-data levels but demonstrating the method's potential. Detection power at real c_n levels is only 0.15 (cannot detect what isn't there), but jumps to 0.92 at injected 1.5. This experiment redeems the method theoretically while confirming it's unnecessary on current benchmarks.\"\n  }\n]","numLines":1123,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1123}}}
